const COMBINED_QUESTIONS = [{"id":"ml-1","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In a Fabric lakehouse, which mechanism controls who can open the lakehouse and read its data through paths other than the SQL analytics endpoint?","options":["Only DAX row-level security roles","Workspace roles and item permissions","Power BI report filters","Sensitivity labels alone"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Workspace & Item Access","sub":null,"explanation":"Access to a lakehouse and its data through paths such as Spark is governed by workspace roles and item permissions. SQL granular permissions on the SQL analytics endpoint only apply when data is accessed through that endpoint, not through Spark or other tools.","source":{"title":"What is the SQL analytics endpoint for a lakehouse? (Security)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint#security"}},{"id":"ml-2","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which feature lets you restrict access to specific tables, columns, or rows when data is queried through the SQL analytics endpoint of a lakehouse?","options":["SQL granular permissions","Power BI deployment pipelines","OneLake shortcuts","The Real-Time hub"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Granular Security (RLS/CLS/OLS)","sub":null,"explanation":"SQL granular permissions on the SQL analytics endpoint control access to specific tables, columns (column-level), or rows (row-level). These rules only apply when data is accessed through the endpoint — not through Spark.","source":{"title":"What is the SQL analytics endpoint for a lakehouse? (Security)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint#security"}},{"id":"ml-3","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When you define row-level security (RLS) roles on a Power BI semantic model, which users does the row filtering actually restrict?","options":["Admins, Members, and Contributors","Only users with Viewer permissions","Every user including the model author","Only external guest users"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Semantic Model RLS","sub":null,"explanation":"RLS filters restrict data only for users with Viewer permissions on the workspace. It doesn't apply to Admins, Members, or Contributors. You define filters as DAX expressions within roles in Power BI Desktop, then add members to the roles in the service.","source":{"title":"Row-level security (RLS) with Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/security/service-admin-row-level-security"}},{"id":"ml-4","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What do sensitivity labels from Microsoft Purview Information Protection provide when applied to Fabric items?","options":["They speed up Direct Lake queries","They guard sensitive content against unauthorized data access and leakage, supporting governance and compliance","They convert tables to Delta format","They create deployment pipeline stages"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Sensitivity Labels","sub":null,"explanation":"Sensitivity labels from Microsoft Purview Information Protection on items help guard sensitive content against unauthorized data access and leakage. They are a key component in helping an organization meet governance and compliance requirements; labeling data correctly ensures only authorized people can access it.","source":{"title":"Microsoft Fabric Workload Publishing Requirements (Sensitivity Labels)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/extensibility-toolkit/publishing-requirements-workload#6-fabric-features"}},{"id":"ml-5","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which statement about endorsing Fabric and Power BI items is correct?","options":["Any user can certify any item","Promotion can be done by anyone with write permissions, but only authorized reviewers (set by the Fabric admin) can certify items","Certification is applied automatically to all new semantic models","Only dashboards can be endorsed"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Endorsement","sub":null,"explanation":"Fabric provides three endorsement badges: Promoted, Certified, and Master data. Any item owner or user with write permissions can promote an item, but only authorized reviewers defined by the Fabric administrator can certify items or label them as master data.","source":{"title":"Endorse Fabric and Power BI items","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/endorsement-promote-certify"}},{"id":"ml-6","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which endorsement badge indicates that a data item is the authoritative, single-source-of-truth data (such as product codes or customer lists) and can only be applied to items that contain data?","options":["Promoted","Certified","Master data","Featured"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Master Data Endorsement","sub":null,"explanation":"The Master data badge indicates the data is a core, single-source-of-truth for the organization. It can only be applied to items that contain data, such as lakehouses and semantic models, and only by users authorized by the Fabric administrator.","source":{"title":"Endorsement overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/governance/endorsement-overview"}},{"id":"ml-7","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In Fabric CI/CD, what is the role of Git integration?","options":["It promotes content between development, test, and production stages","It synchronizes workspace items with a version control repository (Azure DevOps or GitHub) for change tracking and collaboration","It refreshes Direct Lake semantic models","It applies sensitivity labels"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Git Integration (CI)","sub":null,"explanation":"Git integration (continuous integration) synchronizes workspace items with version control repositories such as Azure DevOps or GitHub, enabling version control, branching, pull requests, and change tracking. Deployment pipelines handle the continuous deployment side.","source":{"title":"CI/CD for pipelines in Data Factory in Microsoft Fabric","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/cicd-pipelines"}},{"id":"ml-8","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is a key advantage of using a Power BI project (.pbip) file in Power BI Desktop developer mode over a .pbix file?","options":["It stores the data in the file so reports work offline","It separates the semantic model and report items as text files, enabling source control and concurrent collaboration","It only works with Direct Lake models","It automatically certifies the model"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Power BI Project (.pbip)","sub":null,"explanation":"A .pbip project file (unlike .pbix or .pbit) purposefully separates the semantic model and report items, enabling advanced editing in a code editor, multiple creators working concurrently, source control integration (Fabric Git), and CI/CD. Project files don't contain data.","source":{"title":"Microsoft Fabric adoption roadmap: Power BI project files","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/fabric-adoption-roadmap-mentoring-and-user-enablement#power-bi-project-files"}},{"id":"ml-9","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When you deploy content from one stage to the next in a Fabric deployment pipeline, what is copied?","options":["Only the data in the tables","Only metadata (item definitions); data isn't copied","Both the metadata and all of the underlying data","Nothing — deployment only validates content"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Deployment Pipelines","sub":null,"explanation":"Deployment pipelines (continuous deployment) promote content across Development, Test, and Production stages. Only metadata is copied during deployment; data isn't copied. Each stage is linked to its own workspace.","source":{"title":"Source control and deployment pipelines (Overview)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/graphql-source-control-and-deployment#overview"}},{"id":"ml-10","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A team wants an automated release flow that promotes tested semantic models from a development workspace to a test workspace and then to a production workspace. Which Fabric capability fits this directly?","options":["Git integration alone","Deployment pipelines with Development, Test, and Production stages","OneLake shortcuts","Incremental refresh policies"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Pipeline Stages","sub":null,"explanation":"Deployment pipelines let you create stages (typically Development, Test, Production) each linked to a workspace, and promote items from one stage to the next in a controlled manner. This is the continuous deployment mechanism in Fabric.","source":{"title":"CI/CD for pipelines in Data Factory in Microsoft Fabric (Deployment pipelines)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/cicd-pipelines#deployment-pipelines"}},{"id":"ml-11","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Before deploying changes to a semantic model, a Fabric deployment pipeline shows an orange 'X' between two stages. What does the Review Changes / Change Review screen help you understand?","options":["The capacity cost of the deployment","The difference between the items (e.g., semantic models) in the two stages so you can review the downstream impact before deploying","Which users have Viewer access","The sensitivity label applied to each item"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Downstream Impact Analysis","sub":null,"explanation":"When content differs between stages, the pipeline shows an indicator and lets you Review Changes. The Change Review screen shows the difference between the semantic models in the two stages so you can assess the impact of downstream dependencies before promoting the change.","source":{"title":"Tutorial: Application lifecycle management in Fabric (Compare stages)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/cicd-tutorial#step-11-compare-stages-in-deployment-pipeline"}},{"id":"ml-12","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which tool connects to the read-write XMLA endpoint to perform schema compare and deploy semantic model metadata across environments while retaining incremental refresh history?","options":["ALM Toolkit","The SharePoint admin center","Microsoft Defender XDR","Power Query Online"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"XMLA Endpoint Deployment","sub":null,"explanation":"ALM Toolkit is an open-source schema compare tool for Power BI semantic models. It connects through the XMLA endpoint to deploy metadata across environments and retain incremental refresh historical data, and to diff/merge metadata. Write operations require the read-write XMLA endpoint.","source":{"title":"Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/service-premium-connect-tools#client-applications-and-tools"}},{"id":"ml-13","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What does a Power BI template (.pbit) file contain?","options":["The full report data plus the model","Report pages, the data model definition (schema, relationships, measures), and all query definitions — but no data","Only a connection string","Only DAX measures"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Reusable Templates (.pbit)","sub":null,"explanation":"A .pbit template file contains report pages and visuals, the data model definition (schema, relationships, measures), and all query definitions — but not the report data. This makes it smaller and safe to share for promoting consistency and reuse.","source":{"title":"Create and use report templates in Power BI Desktop","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-templates#creating-report-templates"}},{"id":"ml-14","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A BI lead wants to give new report authors a file that, when opened, prompts only for credentials and a table selection against a predefined data source. Which reusable asset fits?","options":["A .pbids (Power BI data source) file","A .pbit template file","A .pbip project file","A deployment pipeline"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Reusable Connections (.pbids)","sub":null,"explanation":"A .pbids file is a Power BI data source file that specifies a single data source. When an author opens it, Power BI Desktop prompts for credentials and shows the Navigator to pick tables — streamlining the Get Data experience without including authentication or schema details.","source":{"title":"Get data in Power BI Desktop (Use PBIDS files)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/desktop-data-sources#use-pbids-files-to-get-data"}},{"id":"ml-15","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which Fabric experience makes it easy to find, explore, and use the Fabric data items in your organization that you have access to?","options":["The OneLake catalog","The Exchange admin center","Power Query Editor","The DAX query view"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"OneLake Catalog Discovery","sub":null,"explanation":"The OneLake catalog makes it easy to find, explore, and use the Fabric data items you have access to, with filtering and search to surface relevant data. Endorsed items are given priority in catalog discovery.","source":{"title":"Governance overview and guidance (OneLake catalog)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/governance/governance-compliance-overview#encourage-data-discovery,-trust,-and-use"}},{"id":"ml-16","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Where in Fabric can you discover all streaming data — eventstreams and KQL tables — across the organization in one tenant-wide place?","options":["The Real-Time hub","The SQL analytics endpoint","The deployment pipeline view","Power BI Desktop"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Real-Time Hub Discovery","sub":null,"explanation":"The Real-Time hub is a single, tenant-wide place for streaming data. Every Fabric tenant is automatically provisioned with it. It lists all the streams (from eventstreams) and KQL tables you can access, so you can discover, ingest, and act on streaming data.","source":{"title":"Introduction to Fabric Real-Time hub","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/real-time-hub/real-time-hub-overview"}},{"id":"ml-17","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"You need lakehouse tables to reference data in an Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 account without copying the data into OneLake. What should you create?","options":["A deployment pipeline","An external OneLake shortcut to ADLS Gen2","A calculation group","A sensitivity label"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"OneLake Shortcuts","sub":null,"explanation":"OneLake shortcuts let you reference data in place. External shortcuts point to sources such as ADLS Gen2, Amazon S3, and others using a cloud connection with stored credentials, making the data accessible in OneLake without copying it.","source":{"title":"OneLake shortcuts (Types of shortcuts)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts#types-of-shortcuts"}},{"id":"ml-18","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When a user accesses data through an internal OneLake shortcut (for example, to another lakehouse), whose identity is used to authorize access to the target data?","options":["The identity of the calling user","Always the workspace admin's identity","A fixed service principal","No authorization is performed"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Internal Shortcut Authorization","sub":null,"explanation":"For internal OneLake shortcuts, OneLake uses the identity of the calling user to authorize access to the target data, so that user must have read permission on the target location. External shortcuts instead use a cloud connection with stored credentials.","source":{"title":"OneLake shortcuts (Internal OneLake shortcuts)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts#types-of-shortcuts"}},{"id":"ml-19","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which low-code Fabric tool uses the familiar Power Query experience to ingest and transform data from hundreds of sources and load it into a lakehouse or warehouse?","options":["Dataflow Gen2","A KQL queryset","The XMLA endpoint","A calculation group"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Low-code Ingestion","sub":null,"explanation":"Dataflow Gen2 provides a low-code Power Query interface to ingest data from hundreds of sources, apply 300+ transformations, and load results into destinations such as a lakehouse or warehouse. It can run standalone or as a pipeline activity.","source":{"title":"What is Dataflow Gen2?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/dataflows-gen2-overview"}},{"id":"ml-20","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A team needs full transactional T-SQL support — DDL and DML including INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE — on data in OneLake. Which Fabric store should they choose?","options":["The SQL analytics endpoint of a lakehouse","A Fabric Data Warehouse","A KQL database","A Power BI semantic model"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Lakehouse vs Warehouse","sub":null,"explanation":"The Fabric Data Warehouse provides full transactional T-SQL with multi-table ACID transactions (DDL and DML query support). The SQL analytics endpoint of a lakehouse is read-only — you can create views/functions/procedures and run SELECT, but to modify data you must use Spark in the lakehouse.","source":{"title":"What is Fabric Data Warehouse? (Data warehousing items)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/data-warehousing#data-warehousing-items"}},{"id":"ml-21","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which Fabric store is the ideal analytics engine for time-based, streaming events of structured, semi-structured, and unstructured data, automatically indexed and partitioned by ingestion time?","options":["A Fabric Data Warehouse","An Eventhouse (KQL databases)","A Power BI .pbit template","A deployment pipeline"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Eventhouse for Streaming","sub":null,"explanation":"An eventhouse is a collection of KQL databases tailored to time-based, streaming events. Data is automatically indexed and partitioned based on ingestion time for fast, complex analytic querying — even during real-time ingestion.","source":{"title":"What is Real-Time Intelligence? (Store and analyze data)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/real-time-intelligence/overview#how-do-i-interact-with-the-components-of-real-time-intelligence"}},{"id":"ml-22","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"How is eventhouse data made available to other Fabric experiences for analytics?","options":["It is exported to email","Data stored in eventhouses can be made available in OneLake, and can be queried via KQL or a managed SQL analytics endpoint for T-SQL","It can only be queried inside the eventhouse itself","It must be copied into a warehouse first"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Eventhouse OneLake Availability","sub":null,"explanation":"Fabric makes data stored in eventhouses available in OneLake for other Fabric processes. You can query it with native KQL and through a managed SQL analytics endpoint for T-SQL analytics, aligned with the eventhouse data model.","source":{"title":"What is Real-Time Intelligence? (Store and analyze data)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/real-time-intelligence/overview#how-do-i-interact-with-the-components-of-real-time-intelligence"}},{"id":"ml-23","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which semantic-model capability lets Power BI query Delta tables stored in OneLake directly, without importing data or running a dedicated refresh cycle?","options":["Import mode","Direct Lake mode","A .pbids connection file","A KQL queryset"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Direct Lake on OneLake","sub":null,"explanation":"Direct Lake mode consumes Delta/parquet files directly from OneLake into the Power BI engine, without importing or duplicating data and without a dedicated semantic model refresh cycle. This OneLake integration combines the performance of Import with the freshness of DirectQuery.","source":{"title":"Power BI semantic models in Microsoft Fabric (Direct Lake mode)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/semantic-models#direct-lake-mode"}},{"id":"ml-24","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In a Fabric warehouse, what is a recommended way to encapsulate a transformation that creates an aggregated table by joining a fact table to a dimension table?","options":["A Power BI report visual","A T-SQL stored procedure","A sensitivity label","A deployment pipeline stage"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Stored Procedures for Transformation","sub":null,"explanation":"Fabric Data Warehouse supports T-SQL stored procedures. A common pattern is to create a stored procedure (for example, populate_aggregate_sale_by_city) that creates an aggregate table and inserts data via a group-by query joining the fact and dimension tables.","source":{"title":"Tutorial: Transform data with a stored procedure in a Warehouse","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/tutorial-transform-data"}},{"id":"ml-25","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which objects can you create on top of Delta tables in the SQL analytics endpoint of a lakehouse?","options":["Views, functions, and stored procedures (but you can't INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE the underlying data)","Nothing — the endpoint only allows SELECT","Tables with full DML support","Only Power BI measures"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"T-SQL Surface (Views/Functions/Procs)","sub":null,"explanation":"In the SQL analytics endpoint you can create T-SQL views, functions, and stored procedures over the Delta tables, but creating/altering/dropping tables and INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE are only supported in the Warehouse, not the lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint.","source":{"title":"T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area#t-sql-surface-area"}},{"id":"ml-26","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which design is the recommended approach for a Fabric warehouse and a prerequisite for enterprise Power BI semantic models?","options":["A fully normalized OLTP schema","A star schema of fact tables and dimension tables","A single wide flat table only","A graph database"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Star Schema","sub":null,"explanation":"A star schema — fact tables surrounded by dimension tables — is the recommended dimensional modeling approach for a Fabric Warehouse and is considered a prerequisite for enterprise Power BI semantic models. It delivers high-performance analytic queries with fewer joins.","source":{"title":"Dimensional modeling in Fabric Data Warehouse (Star schema design)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-overview#star-schema-design"}},{"id":"ml-27","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In a star schema, which table type stores the quantitative measurements (such as sales amounts) together with dimension keys?","options":["Dimension tables","Fact tables","Bridge tables","Integration/staging tables"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Fact vs Dimension Tables","sub":null,"explanation":"Fact tables store measurements associated with observations or events (for example, sales orders), containing dimension keys plus granular values that can be aggregated. Dimension tables describe the entities (products, customers, dates) used to filter and group the facts.","source":{"title":"Dimensional modeling in Fabric Data Warehouse (Star schema design)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-overview#star-schema-design"}},{"id":"ml-28","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A dimension table must preserve historical versions of a member when an attribute changes (e.g., a salesperson moves region). Which SCD type should you implement?","options":["SCD type 1 (overwrite)","SCD type 2 (insert a new versioned member)","No SCD — truncate and full reload","SCD type 0 (delete the row)"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Slowly Changing Dimensions","sub":null,"explanation":"SCD type 2 inserts a new time-based versioned dimension member, expiring the prior version (end-date + IsCurrent flag) so history is preserved. SCD type 1 simply overwrites the existing row, losing history. You should never truncate-and-reload a dimension that uses surrogate keys.","source":{"title":"Dimensional modeling in Fabric Warehouse: Dimension tables","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-dimension-tables#dimension-design-concepts"}},{"id":"ml-29","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"After ingesting raw sales data into a lakehouse, a team wants to remove duplicates, standardize names, apply business rules, and write cleaned results to curated Gold-layer tables without writing code. What should they use?","options":["A Dataflow Gen2 (Power Query)","A calculation group","A deployment pipeline","A sensitivity label"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Aggregate & Enrich","sub":null,"explanation":"Dataflow Gen2 uses Power Query to apply filters, derive columns, aggregate, merge queries, remove duplicates, and apply business rules through a visual workflow, then write the cleaned results to curated lakehouse (Gold) or warehouse tables — all low-code.","source":{"title":"Prepare and transform data in Microsoft Fabric","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/prepare-transform-data"}},{"id":"ml-30","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"You anticipate a dimension attribute (such as product price) that changes very frequently. What does Microsoft guidance recommend?","options":["Implement SCD type 2 with thousands of versions","Move the frequently changing attribute to the fact table (e.g., as a measure if numeric)","Delete the dimension entirely","Store it only in a Power BI report"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Rapidly Changing Attributes","sub":null,"explanation":"For a rapidly changing dimension attribute, guidance recommends adding that attribute to the fact table instead of generating an overwhelming number of SCD type 2 versions. If the attribute is numeric (like price), it can be added as a measure in the fact table.","source":{"title":"Dimensional modeling in Fabric Warehouse: Dimension tables","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-dimension-tables#dimension-design-concepts"}},{"id":"ml-31","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Using only the SQL analytics endpoint, how can you join a Delta table in one lakehouse to a Delta table in a different workspace in a single query?","options":["Export both to CSV first","Use OneLake shortcuts to reference the external Delta tables, then join them in one T-SQL query","It isn't possible without a warehouse","Use a calculation group"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Cross-Database Joins via Shortcuts","sub":null,"explanation":"The SQL analytics endpoint can query across workspaces by using OneLake shortcuts to reference Delta tables in other lakehouses or warehouses, then joining them in a single T-SQL query.","source":{"title":"What is the SQL analytics endpoint for a lakehouse? (What you can do)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint#what-you-can-do"}},{"id":"ml-32","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In a Dataflow Gen2 heavy-shaping scenario, a team filters numeric columns to keep positive values, replaces nulls in a passenger_count column with 1, and maps payment codes. Which engine option can significantly speed up these non-foldable transformations while keeping no-code authoring?","options":["Modern Evaluator","DirectQuery fallback","Calculation groups","The XMLA endpoint"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Handle Nulls / Filter","sub":null,"explanation":"Enabling the Modern Evaluator high-performance execution engine in Dataflow Gen2 can run heavy data-shaping workloads (filtering, null replacement, code mapping) significantly faster — about 1.6x faster than the Gen1 baseline in Microsoft's example — while preserving the no-code Power Query experience.","source":{"title":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: Choose a dataflow strategy","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/decision-guide-data-transformation#scenario-2-heavy-data-shaping"}},{"id":"ml-33","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What is the recommended approach for the surrogate key of a dimension table in a Fabric warehouse?","options":["Reuse the source business key directly","Use the smallest possible integer data type, typically created with an IDENTITY column","Use a GUID string for every row","Avoid keys entirely"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Surrogate Keys","sub":null,"explanation":"Each dimension table should have a surrogate key using the smallest possible integer data type, typically created with an IDENTITY column (available in Fabric Data Warehouse with some limitations). Surrogate keys decouple the dimension from source business keys and support SCD type 2 versioning.","source":{"title":"Dimensional modeling in Fabric Warehouse: Load tables (Surrogate keys)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-load-tables#process-dimension-tables"}},{"id":"ml-34","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which statement about the lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint query surface is correct?","options":["It supports full INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on Delta tables","It is read-only over Delta tables — you can run T-SQL SELECT queries but must use Spark to modify data","It only supports KQL","It requires a separate warehouse to query"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"Visual Query / SQL Endpoint","sub":null,"explanation":"The SQL analytics endpoint operates in read-only mode over Delta tables. You can run T-SQL SELECT statements (and the visual query editor), create views/functions/procedures, and apply SQL security, but to modify data you switch to the lakehouse and use Spark.","source":{"title":"What is the SQL analytics endpoint for a lakehouse?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint"}},{"id":"ml-35","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A user created an external Delta table with Spark code, but it doesn't appear in the SQL analytics endpoint. What is the recommended fix?","options":["Convert the table to CSV","Create a shortcut in the Tables section to make the external Delta table visible to the SQL analytics endpoint","Recreate it as a Power BI measure","Disable the SQL analytics endpoint"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"Delta Table Visibility","sub":null,"explanation":"External Delta tables created with Spark code aren't automatically visible to the SQL analytics endpoint. Creating a shortcut in the Tables section makes those external Delta tables visible and queryable through the endpoint. Only tables in the /Tables folder are exposed.","source":{"title":"Shortcuts in a lakehouse (Supported shortcut sources)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-shortcuts#supported-shortcut-sources"}},{"id":"ml-36","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which Fabric item is used to run KQL queries, view results, and save or share those queries against data in a KQL database?","options":["A KQL queryset","A .pbit template","A deployment pipeline","A Power BI report"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"KQL Queryset","sub":null,"explanation":"A KQL queryset is the item used to run queries, view and manipulate results on data from a KQL database, and to save, export, or share queries. KQL databases live under an eventhouse.","source":{"title":"Fabric terminology (Real-Time Intelligence)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/fabric-terminology#real-time-intelligence"}},{"id":"ml-37","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which two query languages can you use to analyze data stored in a Fabric eventhouse?","options":["Only DAX","Native KQL and T-SQL (through the managed SQL analytics endpoint)","Only Spark SQL","Only Power Query M"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"Querying Eventhouse Data","sub":null,"explanation":"Eventhouse data can be queried in native KQL (Kusto Query Language) and through a managed SQL analytics endpoint for T-SQL analytics. KQL is optimized for structured, semi-structured, and unstructured streaming data.","source":{"title":"What is Real-Time Intelligence? (Store and analyze data)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/real-time-intelligence/overview#how-do-i-interact-with-the-components-of-real-time-intelligence"}},{"id":"ml-38","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which DAX function evaluates an expression for each row of a table and sums the result, making it an iterator function?","options":["SUM","SUMX","CALCULATE","RELATED"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"DAX Iterator (SUMX)","sub":null,"explanation":"SUMX is an iterator function: it takes a table as its first argument and an expression evaluated for each row, then sums the results. Use SUM when you only need to sum a single column without per-row evaluation.","source":{"title":"SUMX (DAX)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/sumx-function-dax"}},{"id":"ml-39","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"You need a measure that returns the running sum of Total Sales by month, restarting for every fiscal year. Which DAX window function is appropriate?","options":["WINDOW with PARTITIONBY","RELATED","USERNAME","FORMAT"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"DAX Analysis","sub":null,"explanation":"The WINDOW function (a DAX window function) returns multiple rows within a given interval and, combined with ORDERBY/PARTITIONBY, computes results like a running sum that restarts per partition (for example, per fiscal year).","source":{"title":"WINDOW (DAX)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/window-function-dax#example-2---measure"}},{"id":"ml-40","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which semantic model storage mode copies data into an in-memory cache at refresh time, giving the fastest query performance but introducing latency between source updates and report visibility?","options":["Import mode","DirectQuery mode","Direct Lake mode","Dual mode only"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Storage Modes","sub":null,"explanation":"Import mode copies data into the model's in-memory cache at refresh time, so query performance is fastest but there's latency until the next refresh. DirectQuery queries the source at runtime (fresh but slower); Direct Lake reads Delta files in OneLake directly.","source":{"title":"Store data in Microsoft Fabric (Semantic model)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/store-data#semantic-model-for-business-logic-and-reporting"}},{"id":"ml-41","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"For analyzing very large datasets with frequent source updates while avoiding data duplication, which storage mode is recommended?","options":["Import mode","DirectQuery mode","Direct Lake mode","Push semantic models"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Choosing Direct Lake","sub":null,"explanation":"Direct Lake combines the advantages of DirectQuery and Import while avoiding their disadvantages — it eliminates the import copy by consuming data files directly, picking up source changes, and is the ideal choice for very large datasets and datasets with frequent updates.","source":{"title":"Power BI semantic models in Microsoft Fabric (Direct Lake mode)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/semantic-models#direct-lake-mode"}},{"id":"ml-42","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"To model a many-to-many relationship between Customers and Accounts (joint account holders), what should you add?","options":["A bridging table that stores one row per customer-account association, related one-to-many to each table","A single calculation group","A composite key on the fact table only","A sensitivity label"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Many-to-Many via Bridge Table","sub":null,"explanation":"The classic many-to-many scenario is modeled with a bridging table (a factless fact table) storing each customer-account association. Create one-to-many relationships from each dimension to the bridge, and set one bi-directional relationship so filters propagate to the fact table.","source":{"title":"Many-to-many relationship guidance","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/relationships-many-to-many#relate-many-to-many-dimensions"}},{"id":"ml-43","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In a composite model, what cardinality is assigned by default to a relationship that goes across two different data sources?","options":["One-to-one","Many-to-many","One-to-many","No relationship is allowed"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Cross-Source Relationships","sub":null,"explanation":"In composite models, any relationship that crosses sources is created with many-to-many cardinality regardless of the actual cardinality (you can change it afterward). Cross-source relationships are limited relationships and behave differently from intra-source ones.","source":{"title":"Use composite models in Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-composite-models#use-composite-models"}},{"id":"ml-44","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which DAX functions are designed specifically to be used together with window functions like INDEX, OFFSET, and WINDOW to control ordering and partitioning?","options":["ORDERBY, PARTITIONBY, and MATCHBY","SUM, AVERAGE, and COUNT","RELATED and RELATEDTABLE","FORMAT and BLANK"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Window Function Helpers","sub":null,"explanation":"ORDERBY, PARTITIONBY, and MATCHBY are special functions used only with DAX window functions (INDEX, OFFSET, WINDOW, RANK, ROWNUMBER). They define the sort order, partitioning, and row-matching that the window functions rely on.","source":{"title":"Understanding ORDERBY, PARTITIONBY, and MATCHBY functions","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/best-practices/dax-understand-orderby"}},{"id":"ml-45","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which DAX function references the measure currently in context inside a calculation item (for example, to wrap it in a time-intelligence calculation like YTD)?","options":["SELECTEDMEASURE()","RELATED()","USERNAME()","BLANK()"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Calculation Groups","sub":null,"explanation":"SELECTEDMEASURE() is used in calculation item expressions to reference the measure currently in context. Calculation groups apply a set of calculation items (Current, MTD, QTD, YTD, etc.) to whatever measure is selected, avoiding the need to author each variant separately.","source":{"title":"Calculation groups (How they work)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/analysis-services/tabular-models/calculation-groups?view=sql-analysis-services-2025#how-they-work"}},{"id":"ml-46","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Why use a dynamic format string (via a calculation group or per-measure) rather than the DAX FORMAT function to conditionally format a measure?","options":["FORMAT is faster","FORMAT returns a string, forcing an otherwise-numeric measure to become text and breaking visuals like charts; dynamic format strings keep the numeric data type","FORMAT can't be used in measures","Dynamic format strings require no DAX at all"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Dynamic Format Strings","sub":null,"explanation":"The FORMAT function returns a string, which forces a numeric measure to be returned as text and can break charts and other visuals that need numeric values. Dynamic format strings conditionally apply a format string while the measure keeps its numeric data type.","source":{"title":"Create dynamic format strings for measures","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-dynamic-format-strings"}},{"id":"ml-47","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What happens to all measures in a semantic model as soon as a calculation group is added?","options":["They are deleted","Power BI reports use the variant data type for all measures","They become DirectQuery-only","Their format strings are removed"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Calculation Group Side Effect","sub":null,"explanation":"As soon as a calculation group is added, Power BI reports use the variant data type for all measures. If all calculation groups are later removed, the measures revert to their original data types. This can affect dynamic format strings that reuse a measure (use FORMAT to coerce back to string).","source":{"title":"Calculation groups (Considerations)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/analysis-services/tabular-models/calculation-groups?view=sql-analysis-services-2025#considerations"}},{"id":"ml-48","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A semantic model needs to grow beyond the default 1 GB in-memory limit on a Fabric capacity. What must be enabled?","options":["Large semantic model storage format","DirectQuery fallback","A sensitivity label","Automatic page refresh"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Large Semantic Model Storage Format","sub":null,"explanation":"The Large semantic model storage format setting lets a model exceed the default 1 GB limit (up to the capacity size or admin-set maximum). It's required for models beyond 10 GB and also improves XMLA-based write performance. The Desktop upload size remains limited to 10 GB.","source":{"title":"Large semantic models in Power BI Premium","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/service-premium-large-models"}},{"id":"ml-49","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"How can you extend a Direct Lake semantic model with additional tables from other sources (Import or DirectQuery)?","options":["It is impossible — Direct Lake models can't be extended","Use Power BI Desktop to build a composite model on the Direct Lake semantic model, adding new Import/DirectQuery/Dual tables or calculations","Only by re-importing all data","By converting it to a KQL database"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Composite Model on Direct Lake","sub":null,"explanation":"Although you can't mix Direct Lake on SQL endpoint tables with DirectQuery/Dual tables in the same model directly, you can use Power BI Desktop to build a composite model on top of a Direct Lake semantic model and extend it with new Import, DirectQuery, or Dual tables and calculations.","source":{"title":"Direct Lake overview (Comparison of storage modes)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview#comparison-of-storage-modes"}},{"id":"ml-50","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which factor most directly improves Direct Lake query performance by letting the VertiScan engine compute results on top of compressed data?","options":["V-Order compression of the Delta tables","Using string data types for keys","Maximizing column cardinality","Disabling all relationships"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Direct Lake Query Performance","sub":null,"explanation":"V-Order compression boosts Direct Lake performance because VertiScan can compute results directly on top of V-Order compressed data, skipping decompression. Using appropriate (integer) data types, larger segment sizes, and lower cardinality also help.","source":{"title":"Understand Direct Lake query performance","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-understand-storage#what-affects-direct-lake-query-performance"}},{"id":"ml-51","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"For a Direct Lake on SQL endpoint semantic model, which situation causes a query to fall back to DirectQuery mode?","options":["Using V-Order compression","Querying a table in the SQL analytics endpoint that enforces row-level security, or exceeding the capacity guardrails","Adding a measure","Enabling automatic updates"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"DirectQuery Fallback","sub":null,"explanation":"With Direct Lake on SQL endpoints, queries fall back to DirectQuery mode when the model queries a view in the SQL analytics endpoint, a table that enforces RLS, or when a Delta table exceeds the capacity guardrails. Fallback can be slower, so design to avoid it.","source":{"title":"How Direct Lake works (DirectQuery fallback)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-how-it-works#automatic-updates"}},{"id":"ml-52","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which statement correctly contrasts Direct Lake on OneLake with Direct Lake on SQL endpoints regarding DirectQuery fallback?","options":["Both support DirectQuery fallback","Direct Lake on OneLake does NOT support DirectQuery fallback (refresh fails / queries error when guardrails are exceeded), while Direct Lake on SQL can fall back","Neither supports fallback","Only Direct Lake on OneLake supports fallback"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Direct Lake on OneLake vs SQL","sub":null,"explanation":"Direct Lake on OneLake doesn't support DirectQuery fallback — if guardrails are exceeded, refresh fails (like Import) and the model can't be queried until the Delta tables are optimized. Direct Lake on SQL falls back to DirectQuery (if enabled) and refresh succeeds with a warning.","source":{"title":"Direct Lake overview (Fabric capacity requirements)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview#fabric-capacity-requirements"}},{"id":"ml-53","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which two reserved, case-sensitive Power Query parameters must you create to configure incremental refresh in Power BI Desktop?","options":["StartDate and EndDate","RangeStart and RangeEnd","MinDate and MaxDate","From and To"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Incremental Refresh","sub":null,"explanation":"To configure incremental refresh you first create two Power Query date/time parameters with the reserved, case-sensitive names RangeStart and RangeEnd, then filter the table on its date column using them. After publishing, the service overrides these to refresh only recent partitions.","source":{"title":"Configure incremental refresh and real-time data for Power BI semantic models","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview"}},{"id":"ml-101","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In Power BI Desktop, how do you define which rows a security role can see?","options":["By writing a DAX filter expression that returns TRUE/FALSE for each row in a table","By applying a sensitivity label","By creating a deployment pipeline stage","By converting the model to Direct Lake"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"RLS DAX Filter","sub":null,"explanation":"RLS roles are defined in Power BI Desktop using DAX filter expressions on a table. The expression evaluates TRUE/FALSE per row; only rows returning TRUE are visible to members of that role. Dynamic rules can use functions like USERNAME() or USERPRINCIPALNAME().","source":{"title":"Row-level security (RLS) with Power BI (Define roles and rules)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/security/service-admin-row-level-security#define-roles-and-rules-in-power-bi-desktop"}},{"id":"ml-102","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What permissions does a user need to create a OneLake shortcut?","options":["Write permission on the Fabric item where the shortcut is created, plus Read access to the target data","Only Viewer permission on the workspace","No permissions are required","Admin rights on the entire tenant"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1A","topic":"Shortcut Permissions","sub":null,"explanation":"To create a shortcut a user needs Write permission on the item where the shortcut is created, and Read (ReadAll) access to the target data the shortcut points to. Shortcuts to external sources might require additional permissions in the external system.","source":{"title":"OneLake shortcut security","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcut-security"}},{"id":"ml-103","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which Git workflow concept lets a developer work in isolation before merging changes into the production-ready main branch?","options":["Feature branches","Deployment stages","Sensitivity labels","Calculation items"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Git Continuous Integration","sub":null,"explanation":"Feature branches are separate from the main branch and allow isolated development without changing main. Changes are proposed via pull requests, reviewed, and then merged. The main branch holds production-ready code.","source":{"title":"CI/CD for pipelines in Data Factory in Microsoft Fabric (Git)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/cicd-pipelines#why-developers-use-ci-cd"}},{"id":"ml-104","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Why might an organization promote or certify a shared semantic model and require teams to use it?","options":["To make trustworthy, reusable data more discoverable so consumers build reports on a single source of truth","To prevent anyone from building reports","To delete downstream reports automatically","To disable row-level security"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Shared Semantic Models","sub":null,"explanation":"Endorsing (promoting/certifying) a shared semantic model makes trustworthy, quality data more discoverable and given priority in searches. Data consumers are encouraged to reuse certified shared models rather than recreating data, ensuring consistent, trusted reporting.","source":{"title":"Governance overview and guidance (Endorsement)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/governance/governance-compliance-overview#encourage-data-discovery,-trust,-and-use"}},{"id":"ml-105","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In a deployment pipeline, how are OneLake shortcuts with internal (same-workspace) targets handled across stages?","options":["They are automatically remapped across stages, but the target tables/files aren't created — you must create them in the target workspace","They are deleted on every deployment","They keep pointing to the development target forever","They cannot be deployed at all"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"1B","topic":"Shortcuts Across Stages","sub":null,"explanation":"Shortcuts with internal targets in the same workspace are automatically remapped across deployment-pipeline stages. However, the target tables, folders, and files aren't created automatically — you must create them in the target workspace after deployment. External-target shortcuts keep the same target.","source":{"title":"Lakehouse git integration and deployment pipelines (OneLake shortcuts)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-git-deployment-pipelines#onelake-shortcuts"}},{"id":"ml-106","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which scenario best fits a Fabric lakehouse rather than a warehouse?","options":["Working with structured, semi-structured, and unstructured data using Spark in a medallion (bronze/silver/gold) architecture","Needing full multi-table ACID T-SQL DML transactions","Running KQL over streaming events","Storing only Power BI report definitions"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Lakehouse Use Cases","sub":null,"explanation":"A lakehouse supports structured, semi-structured, and unstructured data with a Spark compute layer over the open Delta format, commonly using the medallion architecture (bronze/silver/gold). The warehouse is the choice when you need full transactional T-SQL DML.","source":{"title":"Greenfield lakehouse on Microsoft Fabric (Architecture)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/example-scenario/data/greenfield-lakehouse-fabric#architecture"}},{"id":"ml-107","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which T-SQL command is a supported way to bulk load data into a Fabric Data Warehouse?","options":["COPY INTO","SELECTEDMEASURE","DATESYTD","WINDOW"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2A","topic":"Warehouse Ingestion Methods","sub":null,"explanation":"A Fabric warehouse can be populated by COPY INTO, pipelines, dataflows, or cross-database ingestion (CTAS, INSERT..SELECT, SELECT INTO). COPY INTO is the T-SQL bulk-loading command. The other options are DAX functions, not ingestion methods.","source":{"title":"What is Fabric Data Warehouse? (Data warehousing items)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/data-warehousing#data-warehousing-items"}},{"id":"ml-108","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"When a source dimension member is deleted, what is the recommended way to handle it in the warehouse dimension table?","options":["Record a soft delete by setting a Boolean column (e.g., IsDeleted) and filter those rows out in reports","Immediately hard-delete the row and all related facts","Truncate the whole dimension table","Ignore it and let the fact table break"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Soft Deletes in Dimensions","sub":null,"explanation":"You generally shouldn't synchronize source deletions by removing dimension rows (it can invalidate related facts). The appropriate approach is a soft delete — mark the member with a Boolean (bit) column like IsDeleted = TRUE, and have reports and semantic models filter out soft-deleted records.","source":{"title":"Dimensional modeling in Fabric Warehouse: Load tables","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-load-tables#process-dimension-tables"}},{"id":"ml-109","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"A self-service analyst wants to build a dimensional-style model directly in Power BI without creating a warehouse. Which tool defines the logic to connect to and transform source data into the model tables?","options":["Power Query","KQL queryset","A deployment pipeline","The XMLA endpoint"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2B","topic":"Self-Service Quasi-Dimensional Model","sub":null,"explanation":"Self-service analysts can create a quasi-dimensional model using Power Query, which defines the logic to connect to and transform source data to create and load the semantic model tables — though Power Query can't manage historical change (SCDs) the way a warehouse ETL process can.","source":{"title":"Dimensional modeling in Fabric Data Warehouse (for Power BI)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-overview#dimensional-modeling-for-power-bi"}},{"id":"ml-110","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which DAX window function returns a single row positioned before or after the current row by a given offset (useful for prior-period comparisons)?","options":["OFFSET","SUM","RELATED","BLANK"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"OFFSET for Prior Period","sub":null,"explanation":"OFFSET returns a single row positioned before or after the current row within the same table by a given offset, commonly used to compare a value to the previous period. It's a DAX window function used with ORDERBY/PARTITIONBY (or MATCHBY).","source":{"title":"OFFSET (DAX)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/offset-function-dax#example-1---calculated-column"}},{"id":"ml-111","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"On the Real-Time hub All data streams page, which columns help you assess the trustworthiness and classification of each stream or KQL table?","options":["Endorsement and Sensitivity","Storage mode and Refresh time","RangeStart and RangeEnd","Precedence and Format string"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"2C","topic":"Real-Time Hub Metadata","sub":null,"explanation":"The Real-Time hub All data streams list includes Endorsement and Sensitivity columns (alongside Data, Source item, Item owner, and Workspace), reflecting the endorsement status and sensitivity label of the parent item so you can discover trusted streaming data.","source":{"title":"Explore All data streams in Fabric Real-Time hub","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/real-time-hub/explore-all-data-streams"}},{"id":"ml-112","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"In a many-to-many dimension model with a bridge table, why must you set one relationship to bi-directional (Both)?","options":["To allow filter propagation to continue all the way to the fact table so measures calculate correctly","To improve compression","To enable Direct Lake fallback","To create a calculation group"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3A","topic":"Bi-directional Filter for M2M","sub":null,"explanation":"With a bridge table, filters from one dimension stop propagating at the bridge unless one relationship is set to bi-directional (Both). Setting one bi-directional relationship lets the filter continue to the fact table, so customer/account balances calculate correctly (and become non-additive as expected).","source":{"title":"Many-to-many relationship guidance","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/relationships-many-to-many#relate-many-to-many-dimensions"}},{"id":"ml-113","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"What does the Direct Lake semantic model 'automatic updates' setting do, and when might you disable it?","options":["It automatically reflects OneLake data changes in the model; disable it when you want to control data changes manually via framing","It deletes the model nightly","It converts the model to Import mode","It applies sensitivity labels automatically"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Direct Lake Automatic Updates","sub":null,"explanation":"Automatic updates (enabled by default) ensure data changes in OneLake are automatically reflected in the Direct Lake semantic model. You disable it when you want to control when data changes appear by manually framing (programmatic reframing).","source":{"title":"How Direct Lake works (Automatic updates)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-how-it-works#automatic-updates"}},{"id":"ml-114","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","question":"Which benefit does an incremental refresh policy provide for a large fact table?","options":["It refreshes only the most recent partitions instead of the whole table, making refreshes faster and reducing resource use","It removes the need for any data source","It guarantees real-time data without DirectQuery","It disables row-level security"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"confidence":"high","set":null,"section":"3B","topic":"Incremental Refresh Benefits","sub":null,"explanation":"Incremental refresh automatically partitions the table and refreshes only the most recent partitions, so refreshes are faster and more reliable and consume fewer resources. It also enables very large models (billions of rows) to grow without a full refresh each time.","source":{"title":"Configure incremental refresh and real-time data for Power BI semantic models","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview"}},{"id":"gen-1","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Fabric analytics engineer needs to grant a business analyst read-only access to a single warehouse table without giving them any access to the other items in the workspace. Which approach follows least privilege?","options":["Add the analyst to the workspace as a Contributor and DENY SELECT on the other tables","Share the warehouse item and grant SELECT on that one table via T-SQL, without assigning a workspace role","Add the analyst to the workspace as a Viewer so they can read every item","Add the analyst to the workspace as a Member and rely on a sensitivity label to block the other tables"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Item permissions are granular: you can share a single item and GRANT SELECT on just the needed object without any workspace role, satisfying least privilege. A workspace role would grant access to every item.","source":{"title":"Secure a Microsoft Fabric data warehouse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/secure-data-warehouse-in-microsoft-fabric/"}},{"id":"gen-2","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In a Fabric workspace, which role can create and edit items but cannot share the workspace itself with other users?","options":["Contributor","Admin","Member","Viewer"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Contributor role can create and edit items but cannot share the workspace. Member and Admin can share, and Viewer is read-only.","source":{"title":"Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/roles-workspaces"}},{"id":"gen-3","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A requirement states that sales analysts must see only the rows for their own region when querying the Sales table in a Fabric warehouse. Which control enforces this at the warehouse layer?","options":["Dynamic data masking on the Region column","Column-level security with DENY on the Region column","A security policy that binds a predicate function filtering rows by region","A sensitivity label of Confidential applied to the warehouse"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Row-level security in the warehouse is implemented with an inline predicate function bound by a SECURITY POLICY, which filters which rows a user sees. Masking and CLS do not filter rows.","source":{"title":"Row-level security in Fabric data warehousing — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/row-level-security"}},{"id":"gen-4","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer must ensure that analysts cannot see the Salary column of a warehouse table at all — the column should not appear in query results. Which control should be used?","options":["Dynamic data masking on the Salary column","A OneLake data access role excluding the Salary column","Row-level security filtering out high-salary rows","Column-level security using DENY SELECT on the Salary column"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Column-level security (a DENY on the specific column) removes the column entirely from what the user can query. Dynamic data masking would still return the column but with masked values.","source":{"title":"Column-level security in Fabric data warehousing — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/column-level-security"}},{"id":"gen-5","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A report must display only the last four digits of a credit card number while still returning the row to every user. Which Fabric warehouse feature is designed for this?","options":["Column-level security","Row-level security","OneLake data access roles","Dynamic data masking"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Dynamic data masking obfuscates values in query results without changing the underlying data or hiding rows, making it the right choice for showing only part of a value like the last four digits.","source":{"title":"Dynamic data masking in Fabric data warehousing — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/dynamic-data-masking"}},{"id":"gen-6","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes column-level security (CLS) from dynamic data masking (DDM) in a Fabric warehouse?","options":["Both remove the column from query results, but only DDM requires a security policy","CLS removes access to the column entirely, while DDM returns a masked value that can be inferred around","CLS masks the value while DDM removes the column","Both hide rows rather than columns"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"CLS is authorization — the column disappears — while DDM is obfuscation that still returns a masked value and can be inferred around, so DDM should be paired with real controls.","source":{"title":"Secure a Microsoft Fabric data warehouse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/secure-data-warehouse-in-microsoft-fabric/"}},{"id":"gen-7","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Row-level security can be enforced in three different places in Microsoft Fabric. Which set correctly names all three?","options":["Warehouse security policy, semantic model DAX role, and OneLake data access role","Warehouse security policy, sensitivity label, and workspace Viewer role","Semantic model DAX role, dynamic data masking, and item permissions","OneLake data access role, deployment pipeline rule, and endorsement level"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"RLS lives in three layers: the warehouse (T-SQL security policy), the semantic model (DAX role), and OneLake (data access role). You match the layer to where the query runs.","source":{"title":"Row-level security in Fabric data warehousing — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/row-level-security"}},{"id":"gen-8","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer configured a OneLake data access role granting a user only the Sales folder, but the user can still see all lakehouse data. What was most likely forgotten?","options":["The user was not given a workspace Viewer role","A sensitivity label was not applied to the lakehouse","The user was not removed from the DefaultReader role","Dynamic data masking was not enabled on the folder"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"When you add a user to a OneLake data access role, you must remove them from DefaultReader; otherwise they retain full access to all data.","source":{"title":"OneLake data access roles — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/security/get-started-data-access-roles"}},{"id":"gen-9","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A lakehouse Finance folder must be restricted so that the restriction is honored no matter which engine queries it — Spark, the SQL analytics endpoint, or a semantic model. Which control provides this cross-engine enforcement?","options":["Column-level security on the SQL endpoint only","A DAX role in each semantic model","Dynamic data masking applied in the warehouse","A OneLake data access role on the folder"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"OneLake data access roles apply RBAC to lakehouse folders and tables and are enforced across all Fabric engines, unlike controls that live only in the warehouse or a single semantic model.","source":{"title":"OneLake security overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/security/data-access-control-model"}},{"id":"gen-10","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"To ensure a OneLake data access role is honored when data is queried through the lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint, which configuration is required?","options":["Switch the SQL endpoint to User's identity access mode","Enable dynamic data masking on the endpoint","Assign every user the Contributor workspace role","Apply a Confidential sensitivity label to the endpoint"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"OneLake security applies across engines, but to honor it on the SQL analytics endpoint the endpoint must be set to User's identity access mode.","source":{"title":"OneLake security and the SQL analytics endpoint — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/security/data-access-control-model"}},{"id":"gen-11","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Fabric analytics engineer must restrict access to entire tables, views, and stored procedures in a warehouse for certain users. Which security control operates at that object granularity?","options":["Object-level security using T-SQL GRANT and DENY","Row-level security","Dynamic data masking","Sensitivity labels"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Object-level security (OLS) is enforced at the warehouse/SQL endpoint with T-SQL GRANT and DENY to allow or deny access to whole objects such as tables, views, and procedures.","source":{"title":"Secure a Microsoft Fabric data warehouse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/secure-data-warehouse-in-microsoft-fabric/"}},{"id":"gen-12","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A semantic model must restrict each regional manager to see only their region's sales, where the region is determined by the signed-in user's identity at query time. Which approach implements this?","options":["Static row-level security with a separate DAX role hardcoded per region","Dynamic row-level security using USERPRINCIPALNAME() in the DAX role's filter","A OneLake data access role on the underlying lakehouse folder","Object-level security hiding the Region column"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Dynamic RLS uses functions like USERPRINCIPALNAME() (or USERNAME()) inside the DAX role filter, typically joined to a users/region mapping table, so a single role adapts to whoever is signed in.","source":{"title":"Row-level security (RLS) with Power BI — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/security/service-admin-row-level-security"}},{"id":"gen-13","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which endorsement level in Microsoft Fabric can be applied only by users authorized by the Fabric administrator, signaling an official trusted source of truth?","options":["Promoted","Recommended","Certified","Verified"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Certified endorsement can only be applied by users authorized by the Fabric admin and signals an official, reviewed source of truth. Promoted can be set by any user with write access.","source":{"title":"Endorsement: Promote or certify Fabric content — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/endorsement-overview"}},{"id":"gen-14","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team lead wants to flag a semantic model as recommended by the team so colleagues can find it easily. The lead has edit permission on the item but is not an admin-authorized certifier. Which endorsement can they apply?","options":["Certified","Master data","Promoted","None — only admins can endorse content"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Promoted endorsement can be applied by any user with write/edit permission on the item, so the lead can promote it without admin authorization.","source":{"title":"Endorsement: Promote or certify Fabric content — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/endorsement-overview"}},{"id":"gen-15","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An organization needs classification labels that stay attached to data when it is exported to Excel or a PBIX file and can enforce encryption. Which capability provides this?","options":["Fabric domains","Sensitivity labels from Microsoft Purview Information Protection","OneLake data access roles","Workspace roles"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Sensitivity labels are defined in Microsoft Purview and applied in Fabric; they persist on export (to Excel, PBIX, etc.) and can enforce encryption.","source":{"title":"Sensitivity labels in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/information-protection"}},{"id":"gen-16","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Where are sensitivity labels defined before they can be applied to items in Microsoft Fabric?","options":["In the Fabric admin portal's tenant settings","In each workspace's Manage access pane","In Microsoft Purview","In the semantic model's DAX role editor"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Sensitivity labels are defined in Microsoft Purview (Information Protection) and then applied within Fabric, where they can propagate downstream to derived items.","source":{"title":"Sensitivity labels in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/information-protection"}},{"id":"gen-17","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A governance team wants to trace how a downstream Power BI report depends on upstream lakehouses, dataflows, and semantic models across a workspace. Which Fabric feature shows these dependencies?","options":["Data lineage view","Deployment pipelines","Endorsement badges","Dynamic data masking"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The lineage view traces dependencies between items, letting governance teams see how reports depend on upstream data sources across the workspace.","source":{"title":"Lineage in Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/lineage"}},{"id":"gen-18","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An enterprise must encrypt OneLake data and warehouse metadata using encryption keys it controls in its own Azure Key Vault. Which Fabric governance capability supports this?","options":["Sensitivity labels","OneLake data access roles","Audit logs","Customer-managed keys (CMK)"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Customer-managed keys (CMK) let an organization encrypt OneLake data and warehouse metadata using its own keys stored in Azure Key Vault.","source":{"title":"Customer-managed keys for Fabric workspaces — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/security/workspace-customer-managed-keys"}},{"id":"gen-19","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A data steward needs to organize related Fabric items into a data-mesh structure so business units can find and own their own data products. Which Fabric governance construct is intended for this?","options":["Deployment pipelines","Domains","Workspace Viewer role","Certified endorsement"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Domains organize items into a data-mesh so business units can group and own their data products; endorsement and pipelines serve different purposes.","source":{"title":"Domains in Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/domains"}},{"id":"gen-20","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During testing, an engineer wants to verify that a warehouse row-level security policy correctly filters the Sales table for a non-privileged user. Which T-SQL approach lets them impersonate that user to confirm the filtered result?","options":["Apply a Confidential sensitivity label and export to Excel","Remove the user from the DefaultReader role","Switch the SQL endpoint to User's identity access mode","Use EXECUTE AS USER to run the query in the security context of that user"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"EXECUTE AS USER runs a query in the security context of a specified user, letting the engineer confirm that the RLS security policy returns only the filtered rows for a non-privileged user.","source":{"title":"Row-level security in Fabric data warehousing — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/row-level-security"}},{"id":"gen-21","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Fabric analytics engineer wants to place a Sales report and its semantic model under version control so they can branch, review, and revert changes. At what level is Git integration configured in Fabric?","options":["At the workspace level, connected to a single branch","At the individual report item level","At the tenant level for all workspaces at once","At the capacity level in the admin portal"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Git integration is configured at the workspace level, and a workspace connects to a single branch of an Azure DevOps or GitHub repo.","source":{"title":"Introduction to Git integration — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/intro-to-git-integration"}},{"id":"gen-22","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You need to choose a source-control provider for a Fabric workspace's Git integration. Which providers are supported?","options":["GitLab and Bitbucket","Azure DevOps and GitHub","Only Azure DevOps","Any Git server reachable by HTTPS"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Fabric Git integration supports Azure DevOps and GitHub (cloud) as providers.","source":{"title":"Introduction to Git integration — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/intro-to-git-integration"}},{"id":"gen-23","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A workspace connected to Git contains a semantic model, a report, a notebook, and one item type that Git integration does not support. What happens to the unsupported item when you commit?","options":["The commit fails until the unsupported item is deleted","The unsupported item is deleted from the workspace","The unsupported item is ignored — not synced and not deleted","The unsupported item is converted to a supported type automatically"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Unsupported items are simply ignored by Git integration; they are neither synced to the repo nor removed from the workspace.","source":{"title":"Manage Git branches — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/manage-branches"}},{"id":"gen-24","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After editing a measure directly in a Fabric workspace, an engineer wants those changes reflected in the connected Git branch. Which action moves changes from the workspace to Git?","options":["Update","Sync","Deploy","Commit"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Commit pushes changes from the workspace to Git; Update pulls changes from Git into the workspace.","source":{"title":"Introduction to Git integration — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/intro-to-git-integration"}},{"id":"gen-25","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A teammate has committed a changed measure to the shared branch. In your own workspace connected to the same branch, which action pulls their change into your workspace?","options":["Update","Commit","Deploy","Publish"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Update brings changes from Git into the workspace; Commit is the reverse direction.","source":{"title":"Introduction to Git integration — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/git-integration/intro-to-git-integration"}},{"id":"gen-26","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An analytics engineer wants a Power BI report and semantic model stored as plain-text files so they can be diffed and reviewed in a pull request. Which save format produces source-control-friendly files?","options":[".pbix",".pbit",".pbip",".pbids"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The .pbip (Power BI Project) format saves plain-text files (PBIR report + TMDL model) that are source-control and CI/CD ready.","source":{"title":"Power BI Desktop projects (.pbip) — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-overview"}},{"id":"gen-27","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"When you save a Power BI Desktop file as a project (.pbip), which folders and pointer are produced?","options":["A single binary file containing report, model, and data","Only a Name.SemanticModel folder with the model metadata","A .zip archive that must be extracted before editing","A Name.Report folder (PBIR), a Name.SemanticModel folder (TMDL), a .pbip pointer, and a .gitignore"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Saving as .pbip creates a Name.Report folder (PBIR), a Name.SemanticModel folder (TMDL), a .pbip pointer file, and a .gitignore.","source":{"title":"Power BI Desktop projects (.pbip) — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-overview"}},{"id":"gen-28","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Before the .pbip (Power BI Project) save option appears in Power BI Desktop, what must the engineer do?","options":["Enable it under Preview features","Purchase a Fabric capacity","Connect Desktop to a Git repo","Install Tabular Editor"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Power BI Project (.pbip) format must be turned on under Preview features in Power BI Desktop options.","source":{"title":"Power BI Desktop projects (.pbip) — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-overview"}},{"id":"gen-29","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A colleague asks the difference between a .pbix and a .pbip file. Which statement is correct?","options":[".pbix is source metadata files; .pbip is a single binary with data","Both are binary; only the extension differs",".pbix is a single binary file that includes data; .pbip is plain-text source files (metadata)",".pbip can only be opened in Tabular Editor, never in Power BI Desktop"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":".pbix is a single binary file that can contain data, while .pbip stores plain-text source (metadata) files; you can convert either way via Save As.","source":{"title":"Power BI Desktop projects (.pbip) — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-overview"}},{"id":"gen-30","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer needs to promote content from a Development workspace to Test and then Production within the Fabric service, cloning items forward at each stage. Which feature does this?","options":["Git integration","The XMLA endpoint","Deployment pipelines","Impact analysis"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Deployment pipelines promote (clone) content between stages such as Development, Test, and Production inside the Fabric service.","source":{"title":"Introduction to deployment pipelines — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/intro-to-deployment-pipelines"}},{"id":"gen-31","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A deployment pipeline is being designed. How many stages can it have, and what is the default?","options":["2 to 10 stages, default 3","1 to 5 stages, default 2","3 fixed stages, no customization","Unlimited stages, default 4"],"correct":[0],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A deployment pipeline can have 2 to 10 stages, with a default of 3 (Development, Test, Production).","source":{"title":"Introduction to deployment pipelines — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/intro-to-deployment-pipelines"}},{"id":"gen-32","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During a deployment, an item in the source stage has no matching paired item in the target stage. What is the result?","options":["The deployment is blocked until pairing is set manually","The item is skipped and logged as a warning","The nearest same-named item is overwritten regardless of pairing","A duplicate item is created in the target stage"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"An unpaired item creates a duplicate in the target stage, even if an item of the same name and type already exists there.","source":{"title":"Understand the deployment process — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/understand-the-deployment-process"}},{"id":"gen-33","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You want Test and Production stages to automatically point at their own data sources without editing the semantic model each time you deploy. What should you configure?","options":["A separate Git branch per stage","Deployment rules on the pipeline stages","Read-write XMLA on each workspace","A .pbids file for each environment"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Deployment rules (such as data-source or parameter rules) make each stage point at the correct data automatically, so prod uses prod data.","source":{"title":"Create deployment rules — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/create-rules"}},{"id":"gen-34","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Two paired items sit on the same line in a deployment pipeline. Which statement about paired items is true?","options":["Paired items must have identical names in both stages","Pairing only works for reports, not semantic models","Paired items can have different names across the stages","Pairing is created by committing to Git"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Paired items are linked to the same item in the adjacent stage and can have different names; pairing forms on workspace assignment or a clean deploy.","source":{"title":"Understand the deployment process — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/understand-the-deployment-process"}},{"id":"gen-35","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Before republishing a widely used semantic model, an engineer wants to know which downstream reports and dashboards would be affected and notify their owners. Which capability should they use?","options":["Deployment rules","Impact analysis","Git conflict resolution","XMLA read-write"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Impact analysis shows which downstream reports and dashboards depend on the model so owners can be notified before a change is republished.","source":{"title":"Semantic model impact analysis — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/collaborate-share/service-dataset-impact-analysis"}},{"id":"gen-36","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer wants to trace how a data source flows into a semantic model and then into downstream reports and an app, across a whole workspace. Which view shows these end-to-end dependencies?","options":["Deployment pipeline view","Git history","Lineage view","Capacity metrics app"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Lineage view traces dependencies across items, from source to model to report to app.","source":{"title":"Data lineage — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/lineage"}},{"id":"gen-37","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A developer wants to deploy and manage a semantic model programmatically using SSMS, Tabular Editor, or the ALM Toolkit. Which connection surface do these tools use?","options":["The Power BI REST API","The OneLake Files API","The Git commit API","The XMLA endpoint"],"correct":[3],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"SSMS, Tabular Editor, and the ALM Toolkit connect through the XMLA endpoint to deploy and manage semantic models programmatically.","source":{"title":"Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools"}},{"id":"gen-38","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer connects to a semantic model over XMLA but can only run queries — no metadata changes deploy. What is required to enable management and deployment operations?","options":["Upgrade Power BI Desktop to the latest version","An admin must enable Read-write for the XMLA Endpoint in Capacity settings","Save the model as a .pbit template first","Assign the workspace to a deployment pipeline"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The XMLA endpoint is read-only (query) by default; an admin must enable Read-write under Capacity settings, Workloads, XMLA Endpoint for management and deploy operations.","source":{"title":"Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools"}},{"id":"gen-39","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After performing an XMLA write operation on a semantic model that was originally authored in Power BI Desktop, a colleague tries to download it back as a PBIX and fails. Why?","options":["PBIX download requires a Fabric capacity that was removed","The report was saved as a .pbit, which cannot be downloaded","An XMLA write on a Desktop-authored model blocks downloading it back as a PBIX","Git integration deleted the original binary"],"correct":[2],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"An XMLA write on a Desktop-authored model requires enhanced metadata and blocks downloading the model back as a PBIX, so keeping the original .pbix is important.","source":{"title":"Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools"}},{"id":"gen-40","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team wants to hand a new analyst a ready-to-connect Power BI starting point that includes the report layout, model, and queries but contains no data. Which reusable asset fits?","options":[".pbids data source file",".pbit template file",".pbip project files","A shared semantic model"],"correct":[1],"skill":1,"sub":"1.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A .pbit template contains the report, model, and queries but no data, giving someone a ready-to-connect starting point.","source":{"title":"Power BI report templates — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-templates"}},{"id":"gen-41","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Fabric analytics engineer must make an ADLS Gen2 folder queryable inside a lakehouse, but the security team forbids duplicating the data and requires that changes at the source appear automatically. Which get-data option meets these requirements?","options":["A Data pipeline with a Copy activity that runs on a schedule","A OneLake shortcut that references the ADLS Gen2 folder in place","A Dataflow Gen2 that loads the folder into a Delta table","A Spark notebook that reads the files and writes them to Tables"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A OneLake shortcut references data in place with no copy and auto-syncs with the source; pipelines, dataflows, and notebooks all physically copy the data.","source":{"title":"Get data with shortcuts in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts"}},{"id":"gen-42","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A T-SQL-focused team needs to run INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE statements and wrap them in multi-table ACID transactions. Which Fabric store should they use?","options":["A lakehouse, using its SQL analytics endpoint","An eventhouse (KQL database)","A warehouse","A OneLake shortcut to a Delta table"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The warehouse supports full T-SQL read/write DML and multi-table ACID transactions; the lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint is read-only.","source":{"title":"What is a data warehouse in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/data-warehousing"}},{"id":"gen-43","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team of Spark engineers must ingest and transform terabytes of unstructured and semi-structured files using PySpark and notebooks. Which Fabric store best fits this workload?","options":["A warehouse","A SQL database","A Cosmos DB in Fabric","A lakehouse"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A lakehouse is designed for data engineering and ML over un/semi/structured data using Spark (PySpark, Spark SQL) and notebooks.","source":{"title":"What is a lakehouse in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-overview"}},{"id":"gen-44","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer creates a OneLake shortcut in a lakehouse and later deletes the shortcut. What happens to the underlying source data that the shortcut pointed to?","options":["The source data is also deleted because the shortcut owns it","The source is moved into the lakehouse Files folder","The source data is unaffected because a shortcut is only a symbolic link","The source data is converted to Delta and left in place"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Shortcuts behave like symbolic links and are independent objects; deleting a shortcut leaves the target unaffected.","source":{"title":"OneLake shortcuts — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts"}},{"id":"gen-45","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A self-service analyst wants to clean, filter, and reshape data with Power Query while it is being loaded into a lakehouse, using a low-code experience. Which get-data method should they choose?","options":["Dataflow Gen2","A Data pipeline Copy activity","A OneLake shortcut","Database mirroring"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Dataflow Gen2 uses Power Query to transform data on load, which suits self-service shaping and cleaning during ingest.","source":{"title":"Dataflow Gen2 in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/dataflows-gen2-overview"}},{"id":"gen-46","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer needs to keep an external operational database continuously replicated into Fabric in near-real-time, without building and maintaining ETL. Which capability should they use?","options":["Mirroring","A scheduled Dataflow Gen2 refresh","An external OneLake shortcut to the database files","A nightly Data pipeline Copy activity"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Mirroring creates a near-real-time replica of an external database in Fabric without ETL, keeping the operational data live.","source":{"title":"What is Mirroring in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/database/mirrored-database/overview"}},{"id":"gen-47","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In a lakehouse, an engineer tries to create a shortcut to a Delta table under a subfolder inside the Tables area. Where can Tables shortcuts actually be created?","options":["Anywhere within the Tables hierarchy, including nested subfolders","Only inside the Files area, never Tables","Only after manually registering the table schema first","At the top level of Tables only, where Delta targets are auto-recognized as tables"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Lakehouse Tables shortcuts are top-level only, and Delta targets are automatically recognized as tables; Files shortcuts can be placed anywhere.","source":{"title":"Create a OneLake shortcut — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/create-onelake-shortcut"}},{"id":"gen-48","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A high-concurrency operational application needs OLTP-style writes with foreign keys and relational integrity, backed by T-SQL. Which Fabric store is the best fit?","options":["A lakehouse","An eventhouse","A warehouse","A SQL database in Fabric"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A SQL database in Fabric targets operational OLTP workloads with high concurrency and foreign keys using T-SQL.","source":{"title":"SQL database in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/database/sql/overview"}},{"id":"gen-49","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An analytics engineer must ingest high-volume IoT telemetry as time-series JSON and run high-granularity, interactive analytics with KQL. Which Fabric store is designed for this?","options":["A warehouse","An eventhouse (KQL database)","A lakehouse","A Cosmos DB in Fabric"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"An eventhouse is built for streaming/telemetry and high-granularity interactive analytics over event/time-series data using KQL.","source":{"title":"Eventhouse overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/eventhouse"}},{"id":"gen-50","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A tenant needs one central surface to discover streaming data sources across the whole organization and connect to sources such as Kafka, Event Hubs, and IoT Hub. Which Fabric surface is auto-provisioned for this?","options":["The Real-Time hub","The OneLake catalog","The Monitoring hub","The Admin portal"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Real-Time hub is a tenant-wide, auto-provisioned home for streaming data that lists streams and offers connectors like Kafka, Event Hubs, and IoT Hub.","source":{"title":"Real-Time hub overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-hub/real-time-hub-overview"}},{"id":"gen-51","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer must schedule a large, recurring bulk copy of data from an external SQL database into a Fabric warehouse, orchestrated with a low/no-code experience. Which get-data option is most appropriate?","options":["A OneLake shortcut to the external database","An eventstream","A Data pipeline with a Copy activity","Direct Lake over the source"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A data pipeline Copy activity is designed for scheduled, large bulk copies and orchestration between stores in a low/no-code way.","source":{"title":"Data pipelines in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/create-first-pipeline-with-sample-data"}},{"id":"gen-52","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which statement correctly describes how data lands in OneLake regardless of whether a lakehouse, warehouse, or eventhouse is used?","options":["Each store uses a proprietary format that only its own engine can read","All stores expose data in OneLake as open Delta, so any engine can cross-query it","Data must be exported to Parquet manually before other engines can read it","Only the lakehouse writes to OneLake; the others keep data in isolated storage"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"All Fabric stores expose data in OneLake as Delta by default, enabling any engine to read across them via three-part-name cross-queries.","source":{"title":"OneLake, the OneDrive for data — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/onelake-overview"}},{"id":"gen-53","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer enables OneLake availability on an eventhouse so that Spark and Direct Lake can consume the KQL data. In what form is the eventhouse data exposed in OneLake?","options":["As CSV files that must be reloaded","As read-only KQL result sets","As a DirectQuery connection back to the KQL engine","As open Delta tables, with no copy required"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Eventhouse OneLake availability exposes KQL data in OneLake as Delta so Spark, SQL, and Direct Lake can read it with no copy.","source":{"title":"Eventhouse OneLake availability — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/event-house-onelake-availability"}},{"id":"gen-54","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A semantic model must read the latest Delta data directly from OneLake, achieving Import-like query speed without scheduling a refresh copy. Which mode should the engineer use?","options":["Import mode","Direct Lake mode","DirectQuery mode against the SQL endpoint","Dual storage mode"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Direct Lake reads Delta files straight from OneLake, combining Import-level speed with DirectQuery-level freshness and no refresh copy.","source":{"title":"Direct Lake overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}},{"id":"gen-55","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An internal OneLake shortcut is queried by an analyst. Which identity is used to access the shortcut's target data by default?","options":["The calling user's identity","The Fabric capacity's managed identity","The workspace administrator's identity","A shared service principal with no user context"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"An internal shortcut uses the calling user's identity; Direct Lake over SQL in Delegated identity mode is the exception that passes the item owner's identity instead.","source":{"title":"OneLake shortcuts security — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts"}},{"id":"gen-56","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer must create a shortcut to an on-premises storage location that sits behind a corporate firewall. Which component is required to establish that external shortcut?","options":["The on-premises data gateway","A Fabric eventstream","A Cosmos DB linked service","Database mirroring"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"External shortcuts to on-premises sources are created through the on-premises data gateway.","source":{"title":"OneLake shortcuts — external sources — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts"}},{"id":"gen-57","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In a KQL database, an engineer creates a shortcut to external Delta data. Which KQL construct is used to query data exposed through that shortcut?","options":["The shortcut() operator","The external_table() function","The mv-expand operator","The materialize() function"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Shortcuts created in KQL databases are queried using the external_table() function.","source":{"title":"external_table() function in KQL — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/external-table-function"}},{"id":"gen-58","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer needs one place to browse, explore, and govern every Fabric item, surfacing endorsement, lineage, and sensitivity. Which surface should they use?","options":["The Real-Time hub","The Monitoring hub","The OneLake catalog","The deployment pipeline view"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The OneLake catalog is the single place to browse, explore, and govern every Fabric item, where endorsement, lineage, and sensitivity are surfaced.","source":{"title":"OneLake catalog — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/onelake-catalog-overview"}},{"id":"gen-59","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which requirement is the strongest reason to bring data in with a Spark notebook rather than a Dataflow Gen2?","options":["The transformation is a simple column rename that a business analyst will maintain","The team wants a fully low-code Power Query experience","The workload needs complex, custom code-first transforms and ML at any scale","The goal is only to reference source data without copying it"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A Spark notebook is the code-first option for complex or custom transforms and ML at any scale, whereas Dataflow Gen2 targets low-code Power Query ETL.","source":{"title":"Use notebooks to load data into a lakehouse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-notebook-load-data"}},{"id":"gen-60","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team must continuously ingest a stream of event and telemetry data as it arrives, rather than running scheduled batch copies. Which get-data option is designed for continuous streaming ingest?","options":["A Data pipeline Copy activity","A Dataflow Gen2","A OneLake shortcut","An eventstream"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"An eventstream is the streaming-ingest option for continuous event and telemetry data; pipelines and dataflows are batch-oriented and shortcuts only reference data.","source":{"title":"Fabric eventstreams overview — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/event-streams/overview"}},{"id":"gen-61","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Fabric analytics engineer is modeling a Sales fact table and a Product dimension table with a one-to-many relationship between them. In this relationship, which side is always the fact table?","options":["The 'one' side","The 'many' side","Whichever side has the surrogate key","Either side, depending on the refresh order"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Cardinality sets the table type: the 'one' side is always a dimension and the 'many' side is always a fact. A Product row (one) relates to many Sales rows (many).","source":{"title":"Design a dimensional model in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-62","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A customer's home region changes and the business only ever wants to report on the customer's current region — no history of prior regions is required. Which slowly changing dimension approach fits this requirement?","options":["SCD Type 1 — overwrite the existing value","SCD Type 2 — add a new versioned row with a surrogate key","A bridge table linking old and new regions","A role-playing dimension with USERELATIONSHIP"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"SCD Type 1 overwrites the attribute so only the latest value is kept; a normal (non-incremental) refresh achieves this. History is not preserved.","source":{"title":"Load data into a dimensional model — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-63","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A dimensional model must preserve the full history of a customer's region so that historical sales stay attributed to the region that was in effect at the time of each sale. Which dimension design implements this?","options":["Overwrite the region column on each refresh","Store the region as a degenerate dimension on the fact table","SCD Type 2 — a surrogate key plus StartDate/EndDate (and an IsCurrent flag)","A junk dimension consolidating the region flags"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"SCD Type 2 versions history using a surrogate key and a date range (StartDate/EndDate, often an IsCurrent flag), so each fact row links to the correct historical version.","source":{"title":"Slowly changing dimensions in a dimensional model — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-64","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A semantic model has a single Date dimension that must filter a Sales fact by both OrderDate and ShipDate. There are two relationships from Date to Sales, but only one can be active. How should the engineer analyze sales by ShipDate?","options":["Delete the OrderDate relationship and keep only ShipDate","Merge OrderDate and ShipDate into one column","Create a many-to-many relationship between Date and Sales","Use USERELATIONSHIP in a measure to activate the inactive ShipDate relationship (or duplicate the Date table)"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"This is a role-playing dimension: keep multiple relationships (one active) and use USERELATIONSHIP in a measure to invoke the inactive one, or duplicate the Date table per role.","source":{"title":"Work with role-playing dimensions — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-65","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A model must represent that each salesperson can serve many regions and each region can be served by many salespeople. What is the recommended pattern to resolve this relationship in a star schema?","options":["Denormalize region into the salesperson dimension","A bridge table (a factless fact holding the two keys)","An SCD Type 2 dimension on salesperson","A degenerate dimension on the Sales fact"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A many-to-many between two dimensions is best modeled with a bridge table — a factless fact that stores the two keys and resolves the relationship.","source":{"title":"Model many-to-many relationships — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-66","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Product dimension being loaded into a lakehouse has no single column that uniquely identifies each row, but it must sit on the 'one' side of a relationship. What should the engineer add?","options":["An IsCurrent flag","A bridge table","A surrogate key (for example, a Power Query index column)","A degenerate dimension column on the fact"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"When a dimension lacks a single unique column, add a surrogate key (such as a Power Query index) so it can serve as the unique 'one' side of the relationship.","source":{"title":"Design a dimensional model — surrogate keys — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-67","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Product data is spread across three normalized tables — Category, Subcategory, and Product — forming a snowflake. To follow Fabric star-schema best practice, what should the engineer do?","options":["Denormalize the three tables into a single Product dimension","Keep them separate and add a bridge table between each","Convert the three tables into fact tables","Add a surrogate key to each and leave the snowflake as-is"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A star schema prefers a denormalized single dimension: collapsing Category/Subcategory/Product into one table yields fewer tables, shorter filter paths, and supports hierarchies.","source":{"title":"Star schema versus snowflake — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-68","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A business analyst needs a reusable, low-code way to ingest and shape data from several sources into a lakehouse, without writing Spark or T-SQL. Which Fabric transformation tool best fits?","options":["A Spark notebook using PySpark","A T-SQL stored procedure in a warehouse","A KQL database query","A Dataflow Gen2 (Power Query)"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Dataflow Gen2 uses Power Query for low-code, reusable ETL — the best fit for self-service shaping without writing code.","source":{"title":"Ingest and transform data with Dataflows Gen2 — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/use-dataflow-gen-2-fabric/"}},{"id":"gen-69","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer must apply complex, custom transformation logic over a very large dataset and prepare features for a machine-learning workflow. Which Fabric transformation approach is most appropriate?","options":["The no-code Visual Query Editor","A Spark notebook using PySpark / Spark SQL","A Dataflow Gen2 for self-service shaping","A warehouse view over the SQL endpoint"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Spark notebooks (PySpark / Spark SQL) are designed for big data, complex or custom logic, and ML preparation.","source":{"title":"Transform data with Apache Spark notebooks — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/use-apache-spark-work-files-lakehouse/"}},{"id":"gen-70","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Fabric warehouse developer wants to perform set-based transformations, creating a new table from a query in a single statement and standardizing logic that other loads can reuse. Which approach aligns with T-SQL in the warehouse?","options":["Build a Power Query diagram in the Visual Query Editor","Write a PySpark script in a notebook","Use CTAS (CREATE TABLE AS SELECT) and INSERT…SELECT with views, functions, and stored procedures","Query the data with KQL in an eventhouse"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"T-SQL in the warehouse is best for set-based transforms via CTAS and INSERT…SELECT, with reusable views, functions, and stored procedures to standardize logic.","source":{"title":"Transform data with T-SQL in a Fabric warehouse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/query-data-warehouse-microsoft-fabric/"}},{"id":"gen-71","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A self-service user wants to quickly build joins and filters over lakehouse tables through the SQL analytics endpoint, using drag-and-drop rather than writing code. Which tool is intended for this?","options":["The Visual Query Editor (no-code, generates SQL under the hood)","A Spark notebook","A KQL query in an eventhouse","A DAX EVALUATE query in the DAX query view"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Visual Query Editor is a no-code, drag-and-drop Power Query diagram over the SQL endpoint that generates SQL under the hood — ideal for quick joins and filters.","source":{"title":"Query data in a lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/query-data-warehouse-microsoft-fabric/"}},{"id":"gen-72","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer wants to build a Type 2 slowly changing dimension entirely inside a Dataflow Gen2 using Power Query alone. Why is this not the recommended approach?","options":["Power Query cannot connect to a warehouse","Surrogate keys are not allowed in dataflows","Type 2 SCDs are only supported in DirectQuery","Power Query alone cannot build SCD Type 2 change detection — load from a pre-built SCD2 table in the data warehouse"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Power Query alone can't build the change detection required for SCD Type 2; the pattern is to load from a pre-built SCD2 table produced by the data warehouse load process.","source":{"title":"Load slowly changing dimensions — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-73","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"The order number is needed to filter and drill into individual transactions, but it does not describe a separate entity worth its own dimension table. How should this attribute be modeled?","options":["As a separate SCD Type 2 dimension","Through a bridge table to the fact","As a degenerate dimension — kept as an attribute on the fact table","As a junk dimension in the semantic model"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A degenerate dimension is a fact attribute (like an order number) used for filtering that is kept directly on the fact table rather than in its own dimension.","source":{"title":"Degenerate and junk dimensions — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-74","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A fact table is surrounded by several tiny dimension tables that each hold only a single yes/no flag. What is the recommended way to simplify the model?","options":["Move every flag onto the fact table as degenerate dimensions","Consolidate the small flag dimensions into one junk dimension","Convert each flag dimension into an SCD Type 2","Create a bridge table joining all the flags"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A junk dimension consolidates several tiny flag/indicator dimensions into a single dimension table, reducing clutter around the fact.","source":{"title":"Design dimension tables — junk dimensions — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-75","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"When designing fact tables in a dimensional model, why is it important to keep a fact table at a single consistent grain?","options":["Because mixing grains in one fact table causes incorrect aggregations and double counting","Because facts must always be stored at daily grain","Because a consistent grain removes the need for dimension keys","Because it lets Power Query build SCD Type 2 automatically"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Facts store events at a defined grain (level of detail); mixing grains in one fact table leads to incorrect summarization and double counting, so grain must stay consistent.","source":{"title":"Define fact table grain — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-76","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team wants to enrich and standardize transformation logic in a Fabric warehouse so multiple loads can reuse it — including a saved logical query layer, reusable scalar/table logic, and multi-step ETL routines. Which set of T-SQL objects should they use?","options":["Shortcuts, pipelines, and eventstreams","KQL functions, materialized views, and update policies","Dataflows, semantic models, and DAX measures","Views, functions, and stored procedures"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Reusable T-SQL objects are views (saved query / logical layer), functions (reusable scalar/table logic), and stored procedures (multi-step ETL) used to enrich and standardize.","source":{"title":"Create reusable T-SQL objects in a warehouse — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/query-data-warehouse-microsoft-fabric/"}},{"id":"gen-77","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A dimension is configured as SCD Type 1. Which refresh behavior produces the correct Type 1 result of keeping only the latest attribute values?","options":["An incremental refresh that appends new versioned rows","A normal (non-incremental) full refresh that overwrites existing rows","A refresh that requires a surrogate key and date range","A refresh through a bridge table"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"SCD Type 1 keeps only the latest value; a normal, non-incremental full refresh overwrites existing rows and achieves this behavior.","source":{"title":"Refresh strategies for dimensions — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-78","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A flat sales export contains duplicate rows, missing values, and columns stored as text that should be numeric or dates. Following Fabric guidance, where should this cleansing happen relative to the semantic model?","options":["In DAX measures inside the semantic model at query time","Only in the report visuals after loading","Upstream in the ETL layer (dataflow, notebook, or T-SQL) so the semantic model stays lean","It cannot be cleansed until an SCD Type 2 is built"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Do the heavy shaping — deduplication, handling nulls/missing values, and type conversion — upstream in the ETL layer so the semantic model connects to clean data and stays lean.","source":{"title":"Prepare and cleanse data upstream — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-79","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In dimensional modeling terminology, a bridge table used to resolve a many-to-many relationship is best described as which of the following?","options":["A factless fact table containing the two joining keys","A denormalized dimension with a surrogate key","A degenerate dimension stored on the fact","A junk dimension of consolidated flags"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A bridge table is a factless fact — it holds only the two keys that join the entities and carries no measures — and it is the recommended pattern for many-to-many.","source":{"title":"Bridge tables and factless facts — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-80","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A dataset of billions of IoT sensor readings must be joined, aggregated, and reshaped using custom Python logic before being written back to Delta tables in the lakehouse. Which transformation surface is the best fit?","options":["A Dataflow Gen2 built in Power Query","The Visual Query Editor over the SQL endpoint","A DAX calculated table in the semantic model","A Spark notebook using PySpark and Spark SQL"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Large-scale joins/aggregations with custom code that writes to Delta tables call for a Spark notebook (PySpark / Spark SQL), which handles big data and complex logic.","source":{"title":"Transform data using Apache Spark in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/use-apache-spark-work-files-lakehouse/"}},{"id":"gen-81","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Fabric analytics engineer needs to run ad-hoc T-SQL queries that join two tables already loaded into a lakehouse. The engineer only needs to read the data, not modify it. Which endpoint should be used?","options":["The lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint","A DirectQuery connection from Power BI Desktop","The KQL database query editor","A warehouse T-SQL editor with DML statements"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint exposes lakehouse Delta tables for read-only T-SQL querying, which fits an ad-hoc read-only join.","source":{"title":"Query the SQL analytics endpoint of a lakehouse in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint"}},{"id":"gen-82","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You need to analyze several billion rows of IoT telemetry captured in an eventhouse, summarizing sensor readings into 5-minute buckets over time. Which query language is designed for this workload?","options":["T-SQL over the warehouse","DAX with EVALUATE","KQL (Kusto Query Language)","Spark SQL in a notebook"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"KQL is the language for querying an eventhouse / KQL database and is optimized for real-time, time-series telemetry such as binning readings into time buckets.","source":{"title":"Query data in a KQL queryset — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/kusto-query-set"}},{"id":"gen-83","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A business analyst with no SQL experience must group and sort lakehouse tables to answer a top-products question. They want a no-code experience but the result must still execute as SQL against the SQL analytics endpoint. Which tool fits best?","options":["The KQL queryset editor","A Spark notebook using PySpark DataFrames","The DAX query view","The Visual Query editor"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Visual Query editor provides a no-code Power Query-style diagram experience that generates and runs SQL under the hood against the SQL analytics endpoint.","source":{"title":"Query using the visual query editor in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/visual-query-editor"}},{"id":"gen-84","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In a single T-SQL query run from a warehouse, you must join a fact table that lives in the warehouse to a dimension table that lives in a separate lakehouse in the same workspace. What T-SQL feature makes this possible?","options":["Three-part names for cross-database queries","OPENROWSET against a OneLake path","A linked server definition","The external_table() function"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Fabric supports cross-database queries using three-part names, so a warehouse query can reference tables in another warehouse or lakehouse SQL endpoint in the same workspace.","source":{"title":"Query the SQL analytics endpoint or warehouse with cross-database queries — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/query-warehouse"}},{"id":"gen-85","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A data engineer wants to run analytical SQL directly against Delta tables in a lakehouse from inside a Spark notebook, using familiar SELECT / GROUP BY syntax over the Spark engine. Which approach should they use?","options":["Write KQL cells in the notebook","Use spark.sql() with Spark SQL statements","Attach the DAX query view to the notebook","Call the SQL analytics endpoint from a %%tsql cell"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Notebooks let you run Spark SQL against lakehouse Delta tables via spark.sql() or the %%sql magic, executing on the Spark engine rather than the SQL analytics endpoint.","source":{"title":"Explore the data in your lakehouse with a notebook — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-notebook-explore"}},{"id":"gen-86","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A report author needs to write an analytical query that returns a table respecting the measures and relationships defined in a published semantic model. Which language and command should they use?","options":["Spark SQL with a SELECT statement","KQL with a summarize operator","DAX with an EVALUATE statement","T-SQL with a SELECT statement"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Analytical queries against a semantic model are written in DAX; EVALUATE in the DAX query view returns a table that honors the model's measures and relationships.","source":{"title":"DAX queries — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/dax-queries"}},{"id":"gen-87","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer attempts to run an INSERT statement against a lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint to correct a few rows and it fails. What is the reason?","options":["INSERT requires enabling row-level security first","The SQL analytics endpoint of a lakehouse is read-only","The table must be converted from Parquet to Delta","DML is only allowed inside a notebook cell"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint supports read-only T-SQL; to write data you modify the underlying Delta tables (for example via Spark) or use a warehouse, which supports full DML.","source":{"title":"Better together - the lakehouse and warehouse in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/get-started-lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint"}},{"id":"gen-88","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You need to query a shortcut that points to external data from within a KQL database, treating it like a table. Which KQL function should you use?","options":["external_table()","openrowset()","mv-expand()","materialized_view()"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"In KQL, external_table() lets you query data exposed through a shortcut/external table as if it were a native table in the eventhouse.","source":{"title":"external_table() function — Kusto Query Language — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/external-table-function"}},{"id":"gen-89","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team must let report consumers switch which dimension a chart breaks down by (product, region, or channel) at run time, without editing the report. Separately, they also occasionally need to run table-returning queries against the model. Which pairing correctly matches these two needs?","options":["Calculation groups for switching dimensions; KQL for table queries","Field parameters for switching dimensions; DAX EVALUATE for table queries","Visual Query for switching dimensions; Spark SQL for table queries","USERELATIONSHIP for switching dimensions; T-SQL for table queries"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Field parameters let report users swap which field a visual shows, and DAX EVALUATE runs table-returning analytical queries against the semantic model.","source":{"title":"Create field parameters in Power BI — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/power-bi-field-parameters"}},{"id":"gen-90","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During data exploration, an analyst opens a lakehouse notebook and wants to quickly preview a Delta table's contents as a Spark DataFrame with the least code. Which statement is the most direct?","options":["EVALUATE 'Sales'","spark.read.table('Sales').show()","SELECT * FROM external_table('Sales')","summarize count() by Sales"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"In a Spark notebook, spark.read.table('Sales').show() loads a lakehouse Delta table into a DataFrame and previews it, a common exploration pattern.","source":{"title":"Explore the data in your lakehouse with a notebook — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-notebook-explore"}},{"id":"gen-91","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer is deciding where a real-time analyst, a data engineer, and a report author should each query. The real-time analyst monitors streaming event data. Which store and language should the real-time analyst use?","options":["A warehouse queried with T-SQL","A semantic model queried with DAX","An eventhouse queried with KQL","A lakehouse queried with Visual Query"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Streaming, high-volume event/log data belongs in an eventhouse, which is queried with KQL for real-time and time-series analysis.","source":{"title":"What is an eventhouse in Microsoft Fabric Real-Time Intelligence? — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/real-time-intelligence/eventhouse"}},{"id":"gen-92","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A warehouse developer needs to reference a table in a different warehouse within the same workspace using the pattern database.schema.table. What is this capability called in Fabric?","options":["Federated linked query","Cross-database query with three-part naming","Shortcut virtualization","Composite modeling"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Referencing another warehouse or lakehouse SQL endpoint in the same workspace via database.schema.table is a cross-database query using three-part names.","source":{"title":"Cross-database querying in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/query-warehouse"}},{"id":"gen-93","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A no-code analyst builds a transformation in the Visual Query editor and wants to confirm exactly how it will run on the engine and reuse it elsewhere. What can they inspect?","options":["The generated SQL statement produced by the visual query","The KQL control command equivalent","The DAX measure expression","The Spark execution plan"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Visual Query editor generates SQL under the hood, and the analyst can view that generated SQL to understand and reuse the query.","source":{"title":"Query using the visual query editor in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/visual-query-editor"}},{"id":"gen-94","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You must compute a running total across dates as a table-returning DAX query against a semantic model. Which DAX construct is intended for such windowed calculations?","options":["The KQL scan operator","The T-SQL OVER clause","The Spark SQL WINDOW clause","DAX window functions such as WINDOW, OFFSET, and INDEX"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"DAX window functions (WINDOW, OFFSET, INDEX, RANK) support running totals and period-over-period calculations within analytical DAX queries over the model.","source":{"title":"WINDOW function (DAX) — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/window-function-dax"}},{"id":"gen-95","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A data engineer wants to explore lakehouse data using both Spark SQL and PySpark in the same interactive session, switching languages per cell. Which Fabric item supports this?","options":["The SQL analytics endpoint query editor","A Fabric notebook","The Visual Query editor","A KQL queryset"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Fabric notebooks support multiple languages per cell (for example PySpark and Spark SQL via %%sql), making them ideal for interactive lakehouse exploration.","source":{"title":"Develop, execute, and manage Microsoft Fabric notebooks — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/how-to-use-notebook"}},{"id":"gen-96","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which statement correctly contrasts the write capabilities of a Fabric warehouse and a lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint?","options":["Both support full read/write DML through T-SQL","The warehouse supports read/write T-SQL DML; the lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint is read-only","The lakehouse endpoint supports DML but the warehouse is read-only","Neither supports T-SQL; both require KQL"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The warehouse supports full read/write T-SQL DML, whereas the lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint provides read-only T-SQL access to the Delta tables.","source":{"title":"Better together - the lakehouse and warehouse in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/get-started-lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint"}},{"id":"gen-97","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An analyst needs to aggregate application log events by severity over the last hour from a KQL database. Which KQL operator commonly performs the aggregation?","options":["GROUP BY","EVALUATE","summarize","PIVOT"],"correct":[2],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"In KQL, the summarize operator aggregates rows (for example count by severity), often combined with bin() on a timestamp for time buckets.","source":{"title":"summarize operator — Kusto Query Language — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/kusto/query/summarize-operator"}},{"id":"gen-98","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You want to run an analytical DAX query interactively, test a measure, and see the returned table without building a report visual. Where in Power BI do you do this?","options":["The DAX query view","Power Query Editor","The Model view relationships pane","The Performance Analyzer pane"],"correct":[0],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The DAX query view lets you author and run EVALUATE-based DAX queries against the semantic model and inspect the returned table directly.","source":{"title":"DAX query view — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/dax-query-view"}},{"id":"gen-99","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team of SQL-fluent analysts wants to join and filter lakehouse tables using code, but must not accidentally modify the source data. Which querying option best matches their skills while enforcing read-only access?","options":["Editing Delta tables directly in a Spark notebook","T-SQL against the lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint","KQL against an eventhouse","DAX EVALUATE against a semantic model"],"correct":[1],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"T-SQL against the lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint suits SQL-fluent users and is inherently read-only, so it cannot alter the underlying data.","source":{"title":"Query the SQL analytics endpoint of a lakehouse in Microsoft Fabric — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint"}},{"id":"gen-100","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Match the task to the right query surface: a data scientist needs to profile a large Delta table with custom Python logic and SQL together before modeling. Which surface is most appropriate?","options":["The Visual Query editor","The KQL queryset","The DAX query view","A Fabric notebook using Spark SQL and PySpark"],"correct":[3],"skill":2,"sub":"2.3","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Profiling large Delta tables with mixed Python and SQL logic is best done in a Fabric notebook, which supports Spark SQL and PySpark together for data exploration.","source":{"title":"Explore the data in your lakehouse with a notebook — Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-notebook-explore"}},{"id":"gen-101","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Fabric analytics engineer is designing a Sales semantic model. Fact tables hold transactions and dimension tables hold Product, Customer, and Date. To follow star schema best practice, how should the relationships between each dimension and the fact table be configured?","options":["One-to-many from each dimension to the fact table, with the dimension key on the one side being unique","Many-to-one from the fact table to each dimension, with the fact key being unique","One-to-one between each dimension and the fact table","Many-to-many between each dimension and the fact table using a bridge table"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"In a star schema the standard relationship is one-to-many from dimension to fact, and the column on the one side (the dimension key) must be unique.","source":{"title":"Design a dimensional model in Microsoft Fabric - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-scalable-semantic-models/"}},{"id":"gen-102","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You need a semantic model that reads Delta tables directly from OneLake to give near-real-time data with VertiPaq (Import-like) query speed, without importing a copy of the data. Which storage mode meets this requirement?","options":["Import","Direct Lake","DirectQuery","Dual"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Direct Lake uses the VertiPaq engine to read Delta files from OneLake, combining Import-class speed with freshness and no imported copy.","source":{"title":"Understand Direct Lake storage mode - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}},{"id":"gen-103","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A model expected to grow beyond 1 GB is being created in the Power BI service. To ensure it can scale past the default size limit, when must the Large semantic model storage format be enabled?","options":["Only after the model exceeds 1 GB during a refresh","At any time, because existing partitions are automatically converted","Before the first refresh of the model","After publishing, but only once report visuals begin timing out"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Large semantic model storage format must be enabled before the first refresh for models expected to exceed 1 GB.","source":{"title":"Large semantic models in Power BI Premium - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-large-models"}},{"id":"gen-104","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A team maintains 20 base measures and must report each one as Current, YTD, PY, and YOY%. They want to avoid creating 80 separate measures. Which semantic model feature applies one reusable set of time calculations across any measure?","options":["Field parameters","A bridge table","Composite model","A calculation group"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A calculation group applies one set of calculation items (YTD, PY, YOY%, etc.) to any measure, eliminating measure explosion.","source":{"title":"Calculation groups in Power BI - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/calculation-groups"}},{"id":"gen-105","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Inside a calculation item such as YTD, which DAX function acts as the placeholder that represents whichever measure the user has placed in the visual?","options":["SELECTEDMEASURE()","SELECTEDVALUE()","VALUES()","HASONEVALUE()"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"SELECTEDMEASURE() is the placeholder inside a calculation item that refers to the measure currently in context.","source":{"title":"Calculation groups - SELECTEDMEASURE - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/calculation-groups"}},{"id":"gen-106","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After adding a calculation group to a semantic model, a developer notices a change in how the model's measures behave in tools that inspect data types. What is the documented side effect of adding a calculation group?","options":["All relationships switch to bidirectional cross-filtering","All measures are converted to the variant data type","The model is automatically upgraded to large semantic model storage format","All Import tables are converted to Direct Lake"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Adding a calculation group turns all measures in the model into the variant data type, since a calculation item can return different result types.","source":{"title":"Calculation groups - considerations and limitations - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/calculation-groups"}},{"id":"gen-107","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A report author wants end users to be able to switch a chart's axis between Product, Region, and Channel without editing the report. Which semantic model feature enables users to swap which field a visual displays?","options":["A calculation group","USERELATIONSHIP","A field parameter","A perspective"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Field parameters let report users switch which dimension or measure a visual shows; they are implemented as a calculated table.","source":{"title":"Create field parameters in Power BI - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/power-bi-field-parameters"}},{"id":"gen-108","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Date dimension has two date columns in the fact table: OrderDate and ShipDate. Only one relationship between Date and the fact can be active at a time. How should a measure compute totals by ShipDate when the active relationship uses OrderDate?","options":["Set both relationships to active and rely on cross-filtering","Delete the OrderDate relationship so ShipDate becomes active","Use a bidirectional cross-filter on the ShipDate relationship","Wrap the calculation in CALCULATE and activate the ShipDate relationship with USERELATIONSHIP"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"When multiple relationships exist, one is active and the others are inactive; USERELATIONSHIP inside CALCULATE activates the inactive relationship for that measure.","source":{"title":"USERELATIONSHIP function (DAX) - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/userelationship-function-dax"}},{"id":"gen-109","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A composite model contains a fact table in Import mode and another fact table in DirectQuery mode, and both must filter through a single shared Date dimension. Which storage mode should the shared Date dimension use so it serves both facts efficiently?","options":["Dual","Import","DirectQuery","Direct Lake"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Dual storage mode lets a shared dimension behave as Import or DirectQuery per query, which is ideal for shared dimensions in composite models.","source":{"title":"Storage mode in Power BI Desktop - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-storage-mode"}},{"id":"gen-110","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Sales facts relate to Product, and a separate table lists the many Promotions each Product can belong to, where each Promotion also covers many Products. What is the recommended modeling approach to relate Products and Promotions?","options":["Create a direct many-to-many relationship with single cross-filter","Introduce a bridge table between Products and Promotions","Merge Promotions into the fact table as extra columns","Use USERELATIONSHIP between Products and Promotions"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A many-to-many relationship is best modeled with a bridge table that has a one-to-many relationship to each of the two tables.","source":{"title":"Many-to-many relationships in Power BI - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-many-to-many-relationships"}},{"id":"gen-111","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A developer writes a weighted-average-price measure that must multiply Quantity by UnitPrice for each row and then sum the results. Which DAX approach correctly evaluates the calculation row by row over the fact table?","options":["SUM(Sales[Quantity]) * SUM(Sales[UnitPrice])","CALCULATE(SUM(Sales[Quantity]), Sales[UnitPrice])","SUMX(Sales, Sales[Quantity] * Sales[UnitPrice])","AVERAGE(Sales[UnitPrice])"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"SUMX is an iterator that evaluates the expression row by row over the table before summing, which is required when multiplying two columns per row.","source":{"title":"SUMX function (DAX) - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/sumx-function-dax"}},{"id":"gen-112","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You must compute a running total and a period-over-period comparison in DAX without writing complex FILTER expressions over the whole table. Which family of DAX functions is designed for this?","options":["Information functions such as HASONEVALUE and ISBLANK","Text functions such as CONCATENATE and FORMAT","Relationship functions such as RELATED and USERELATIONSHIP","Windowing functions such as WINDOW, OFFSET, INDEX, and RANK"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Windowing functions (WINDOW, OFFSET, INDEX, RANK) are built for running totals and period-over-period calculations without complex FILTER logic.","source":{"title":"WINDOW function (DAX) - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/window-function-dax"}},{"id":"gen-113","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A calculation group contains a YOY% item that should display as a percentage, while other items in the same group display as whole numbers or currency depending on the base measure. What feature lets each calculation item control its own display format?","options":["A dynamic format string on the calculation item","A field parameter bound to the item","Setting the model's default measure format","A perspective that hides other formats"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Dynamic format strings let each calculation item define its own display format, so YOY% can show as a percentage independent of the base measure.","source":{"title":"Dynamic format strings for calculation groups - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/calculation-groups"}},{"id":"gen-114","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A model has several calculation groups (for example, Time Intelligence and Currency Conversion) that could both apply to the same measure. What must you configure to control the order in which the groups are applied?","options":["The cross-filter direction of each relationship","The precedence of each calculation group","The storage mode of the calculation group tables","A USERELATIONSHIP between the groups"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"When multiple calculation groups exist, precedence determines the order in which they are evaluated and applied.","source":{"title":"Calculation groups - precedence - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/calculation-groups"}},{"id":"gen-115","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"To make Time Intelligence functions such as TOTALYTD work reliably, a Date dimension is added to the model. Beyond creating the relationship to the fact table, what additional step ensures DAX time intelligence behaves correctly?","options":["Set the Date table to DirectQuery storage mode","Enable bidirectional cross-filtering on the Date relationship","Mark the table as a date table","Convert the Date table to a calculation group"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Marking a table as a date table gives DAX time intelligence functions a contiguous date column to operate over, which the star-schema build step calls for.","source":{"title":"Set and use date tables in Power BI Desktop - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-date-tables"}},{"id":"gen-116","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A developer wants to define a variable once, reuse it several times in an expression, and improve readability and performance in a DAX measure. Which construct should be used?","options":["CALCULATE with multiple filter arguments","An iterator such as AVERAGEX","ALL to remove filters","VAR / RETURN"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"VAR/RETURN defines a variable that is evaluated once and reused, improving both readability and performance.","source":{"title":"VAR keyword (DAX) - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/var-dax"}},{"id":"gen-117","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You want to build a composite model that adds local Import tables and custom measures on top of an existing, centrally governed shared semantic model. Which statement about this capability is correct?","options":["A composite model can be built on top of a shared semantic model, mixing it with additional data","You cannot build a composite model on top of a shared semantic model; you must copy the model first","Composite models require all tables to use DirectQuery","Composite models are only supported with Direct Lake on SQL endpoint"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Power BI supports composite models built on top of a shared semantic model, letting you extend a governed model with additional data and measures.","source":{"title":"Use composite models on Power BI semantic models - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-composite-models"}},{"id":"gen-118","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A relationship between a Customer dimension and a Sales fact currently uses single cross-filter direction, but a specific analysis requires the fact to filter the dimension as well. What is the guidance on using bidirectional (both) cross-filtering?","options":["Always set relationships to both directions for maximum flexibility","Use both directions sparingly, because it can create ambiguity and performance issues","Bidirectional cross-filtering is not supported in star schemas","Both directions are required whenever a Date table is present"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Bidirectional (both) cross-filter direction should be used sparingly because it can introduce ambiguity and hurt performance.","source":{"title":"Model relationships in Power BI Desktop - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-relationships-understand"}},{"id":"gen-119","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A measure must return a value only when a single Region is selected in the current filter context, and otherwise return a blank. Which DAX information function most directly tests whether exactly one value is in context?","options":["SUMX(Region, 1)","ALL(Region[Name])","HASONEVALUE(Region[Name])","USERELATIONSHIP(Region[Key], Sales[RegionKey])"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"HASONEVALUE returns TRUE when exactly one value is in the current filter context, making it the right guard for single-selection logic.","source":{"title":"HASONEVALUE function (DAX) - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/hasonevalue-function-dax"}},{"id":"gen-120","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In a Direct Lake semantic model, which of the following best describes how it delivers query performance while keeping data fresh?","options":["It imports all data into VertiPaq and requires a scheduled refresh to update","It sends every query to the source database at runtime like DirectQuery","It caches report visuals but stores no model data at all","It uses the VertiPaq engine to read Delta files from OneLake without importing a copy"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.1","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Direct Lake reads Delta tables from OneLake through the VertiPaq engine, so it achieves Import-like speed with fresh data and no imported copy.","source":{"title":"Direct Lake overview - Microsoft Learn","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}},{"id":"gen-121","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Fabric analytics engineer builds a Direct Lake semantic model and must guarantee it never silently switches to DirectQuery, even if guardrails are exceeded. Which Direct Lake variant meets this requirement?","options":["Direct Lake on SQL endpoint","Direct Lake with a DirectQuery real-time partition","Direct Lake on SQL endpoint in delegated mode","Direct Lake on OneLake"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Direct Lake on OneLake has no DirectQuery fallback — when guardrails are exceeded it fails like an Import model, whereas Direct Lake on SQL endpoint falls back to DirectQuery.","source":{"title":"Direct Lake overview — Microsoft Fabric","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}},{"id":"gen-122","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"In a Direct Lake semantic model, what does the operation known as 'framing' actually do during a refresh?","options":["Loads every column of every table into memory in advance","Rebuilds the VertiPaq compression dictionaries from scratch","Copies only metadata so the model points to the latest Delta files","Runs OPTIMIZE and V-Order on the underlying Delta tables"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Framing is a metadata-only refresh that repoints the model to the newest Delta/Parquet files in seconds; column data is transcoded into memory later, on demand.","source":{"title":"Direct Lake overview — Microsoft Fabric","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}},{"id":"gen-123","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A semantic model on a Direct Lake dataset must honor row-level security that is defined on the lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint. Which configuration honors that SQL RLS, and what is the trade-off?","options":["Direct Lake on OneLake — no trade-off, RLS applies natively","Direct Lake on OneLake — but only in delegated identity mode","Import mode — RLS is copied into the model with the data","Direct Lake on SQL endpoint — RLS queries fall back to DirectQuery"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Direct Lake on SQL endpoint honors SQL-endpoint RLS/OLS/CLS, but any RLS query triggers a fallback to DirectQuery; Direct Lake on OneLake uses OneLake file access and does not apply SQL-endpoint RLS.","source":{"title":"Direct Lake overview — Microsoft Fabric","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}},{"id":"gen-124","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A nightly refresh of a 400-million-row Import fact table takes several hours because it reloads all history every time. Which feature most directly reduces the refresh time and cost?","options":["Switching the model to DirectQuery mode","Increasing the number of visuals per report page","Configuring incremental refresh on the fact table","Enabling automatic page refresh on the report"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Incremental refresh partitions the fact table and refreshes only recent partitions in a rolling window, making refresh faster and cheaper while leaving historical partitions untouched.","source":{"title":"Incremental refresh for semantic models — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview"}},{"id":"gen-125","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You are configuring incremental refresh in Power Query. Which two parameters must you define, and what constraint applies to their names?","options":["StartDate and EndDate — any casing is accepted","MinDate and MaxDate — must be text data type","PartitionStart and PartitionEnd — must be whole numbers","RangeStart and RangeEnd — reserved and case-sensitive"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Incremental refresh requires the reserved, case-sensitive date/time parameters RangeStart and RangeEnd, which are used to filter the fact table so the service can auto-create partitions.","source":{"title":"Configure incremental refresh — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-configure"}},{"id":"gen-126","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"After configuring incremental refresh with RangeStart and RangeEnd, refresh performance is no better than before. Which requirement was most likely not met?","options":["The RangeStart/RangeEnd filter must fold as a query to the source","The model must be published to a Pro (non-Premium) workspace","Automatic page refresh must be enabled on all report pages","The fact table must be converted to a calculated table"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Incremental refresh depends on query folding so the RangeStart/RangeEnd filter is pushed to the data source; without folding, the source returns all rows and the benefit is lost.","source":{"title":"Incremental refresh and query folding — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview"}},{"id":"gen-127","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A report page is slow, but you do not know which visual is responsible. Which Power BI Desktop tool lets you see each visual's DAX query duration and render time so you can target the bottleneck?","options":["VertiPaq Analyzer","Deployment pipelines","The Optimize ribbon's pause visuals","Performance Analyzer"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Performance Analyzer records the DAX query and render time for each visual, letting you identify and fix the slowest one first.","source":{"title":"Use Performance Analyzer to examine report performance — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-performance-analyzer"}},{"id":"gen-128","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A measure recomputes the same base expression several times, causing a slow visual. Which DAX practice most directly improves it?","options":["Convert the measure into a calculated column","Wrap every reference in an additional CALCULATE","Replace the measure with an iterator over the fact table","Store the repeated expression in a VAR and reuse it"],"correct":[3],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A variable (VAR) is evaluated once and reused, eliminating redundant recomputation; calculated columns and extra iterators or context transitions typically make performance worse.","source":{"title":"DAX variables — DAX","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dax/best-practices/dax-variables"}},{"id":"gen-129","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"To maximize Direct Lake query performance, which optimization should be applied to the underlying Delta tables so the VertiPaq engine reads fewer, larger row groups?","options":["Disable V-Order to reduce write time","Run OPTIMIZE with V-Order on the Delta tables","Convert the Delta tables to CSV files","Split each table into many small Parquet files"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Running OPTIMIZE compacts many small files into fewer, larger row groups and V-Order reorders data for faster VertiPaq reads, both improving Direct Lake performance.","source":{"title":"Delta Lake table optimization and V-Order — Microsoft Fabric","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/delta-optimization-and-v-order"}},{"id":"gen-130","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A large dimension column stores highly unique free-text values, bloating the model and slowing queries. Which action best reduces VertiPaq memory footprint?","options":["Add more measures that reference the column","Change the column to a calculated column","Reduce the column's cardinality or remove it if unused","Increase the number of relationships to the column"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"VertiPaq compresses low-cardinality columns far better than high-cardinality ones, so reducing cardinality (or removing unused columns) shrinks the model and speeds queries.","source":{"title":"Optimization guide for Power BI — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/power-bi-optimization"}},{"id":"gen-131","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A Direct Lake on OneLake model must also include a small Import table for budget targets that are maintained in a spreadsheet. Is this possible, and why?","options":["Yes — Direct Lake on OneLake supports composite models with Import tables","No — Direct Lake models cannot contain any Import tables","Yes — but only Direct Lake on SQL endpoint supports composite models","No — you must convert the entire model to DirectQuery first"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Direct Lake on OneLake supports composite models, allowing Import tables alongside Direct Lake tables; Direct Lake on SQL endpoint does not support composite models.","source":{"title":"Direct Lake overview — Microsoft Fabric","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}},{"id":"gen-132","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An analytics engineer wants to enforce best-practice modeling rules (unused columns, missing summarization, high-cardinality relationships) programmatically against a semantic model. Which tool is designed for this and for advanced model editing?","options":["Performance Analyzer","Power Query Editor","Tabular Editor with the Best Practice Analyzer","The Fabric capacity metrics app"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Tabular Editor provides advanced model editing and the Best Practice Analyzer, which scans the model against configurable rules to flag optimization issues.","source":{"title":"External tools in Power BI Desktop — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-external-tools"}},{"id":"gen-133","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"You want to capture and analyze the storage engine vs formula engine time of a slow DAX query, viewing server timings and the query plan. Which external tool is best suited?","options":["DAX Studio","Deployment pipelines","OneLake file explorer","The Fabric SQL analytics endpoint"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"DAX Studio captures server timings (storage engine vs formula engine) and query plans, making it the standard tool for diagnosing slow DAX beyond Performance Analyzer.","source":{"title":"External tools in Power BI Desktop — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-external-tools"}},{"id":"gen-134","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A very large Import fact table exceeds 1 GB. What must be enabled before its first refresh so incremental refresh and large partitions work correctly?","options":["Automatic page refresh","Large semantic model storage format","DirectQuery fallback","Query caching in the Power BI service"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"For models larger than 1 GB you must enable the Large semantic model storage format before the first refresh so the service can hold the larger partitioned model.","source":{"title":"Large semantic models in Power BI Premium — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-large-models"}},{"id":"gen-135","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"A billion-row fact table makes aggregated visuals (yearly totals by region) slow. Which model design technique lets those high-level visuals answer from a much smaller pre-summarized table while detail queries still hit the fact?","options":["Adding more calculated columns to the fact table","Increasing the report's visual count","Configuring user-defined aggregations","Disabling the star schema relationships"],"correct":[2],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Aggregations pre-summarize large fact tables so summary-level queries hit the small aggregation table, dramatically reducing scan cost while detailed queries still resolve against the fact.","source":{"title":"User-defined aggregations — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/transform-model/aggregations-advanced"}},{"id":"gen-136","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"During incremental refresh setup you also want the service to skip refreshing partitions whose source data has not actually changed. Which option enables that behavior?","options":["Detect data changes","V-Order compaction","Query caching","Automatic page refresh"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"The Detect data changes option uses a column (such as a last-modified timestamp) so only partitions that actually changed are refreshed, further reducing refresh work.","source":{"title":"Incremental refresh — detect data changes — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview"}},{"id":"gen-137","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"An engineer needs the freshest data available for the current day in an incrementally refreshed model without a full refresh. Which incremental refresh capability provides this on Premium?","options":["A DirectQuery real-time partition for the latest period","Enabling V-Order on the fact table","Switching the whole model to Import","Reducing the rolling window to zero"],"correct":[0],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Incremental refresh can add an optional DirectQuery real-time partition (Premium) that queries the source live for the most recent data while older partitions stay imported.","source":{"title":"Incremental refresh and real-time data — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview"}},{"id":"gen-138","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Which statement correctly describes how Direct Lake reads data compared with Import mode?","options":["Direct Lake imports a compressed copy of the data on a schedule","Direct Lake reads Delta/Parquet from OneLake via VertiPaq with no imported copy","Direct Lake always queries the SQL endpoint row by row","Direct Lake stores data only in the report's PBIX file"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Direct Lake reads Delta/Parquet files directly from OneLake using the VertiPaq engine, giving Import-class query speed without maintaining a separate imported copy of the data.","source":{"title":"Direct Lake overview — Microsoft Fabric","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}},{"id":"gen-139","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"Direct Lake guardrails (number of files, row groups, rows, and memory) determine when queries fall back or fail. What primarily governs the size of these guardrails?","options":["The number of report pages in the workspace","The Fabric capacity SKU","The number of measures in the model","The report theme selected"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"Direct Lake guardrail limits scale by the Fabric capacity SKU; a larger SKU raises the thresholds before a model on SQL endpoint falls back to DirectQuery (or a OneLake model fails).","source":{"title":"Direct Lake guardrails — Microsoft Fabric","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview"}},{"id":"gen-140","sourceBank":"microsoft-learn","type":"single","generated":true,"question":"To keep a semantic model fast at enterprise scale, which model structure should an analytics engineer favor?","options":["A single flat wide table combining all facts and dimensions","A tight star schema with fact and dimension tables","A snowflake with many chained bridge tables for every attribute","Many bidirectional relationships between all tables"],"correct":[1],"skill":3,"sub":"3.2","confidence":"high","set":null,"section":null,"topic":null,"explanation":"A tight star schema with clean fact and dimension tables is the recommended design for VertiPaq performance; flat wide tables and excessive bidirectional relationships degrade query performance.","source":{"title":"Star schema and the importance for Power BI — Power BI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/star-schema"}}];
